The question really is whether VMS will still be considered worth doing
*anything* with by whoever owns it should such a second migration be
required.  Compaq's own numbers suggest that the Alphacide cut deeply into
Alpha (including VMS) Q3 sales and that the merger discussion certainly did
nothing to help them out of their already-depressed state (Tru64 - though
not VMS - rebounded strongly in Q4, but only to previous levels - and that
seems to have been due to a small number of large sales likely set up some
time ago).

Couple depressed sales with the expressed intent of both Compaq and HP to
phase out everything but Windows and Linux (and perhaps keep HP/UX around
for a while, in HP's case) and regardless of how the merger turns out VMS
looks more and more like toast.  Can the decreasing attractiveness of the
new Itanic home it hasn't yet even moved into do anything but further
discourage people from committing to it?  One really needs rose-colored
glasses to consider this a positive development.

- bill





