Hello folks:

Well, I recently found a few serious bugs to fix (SKPC and RET 
instructions and memory access).  NetBSD/vax now boots normally (square 
one).  SKPC bug cause the 'building database' to crash entire system 
(ffs_nodealloc).  RET bug cause the 'C' shell (/bin/csh) to create clones 
itself instead of execute background evaluation (eval `command`).  The RET 
bug bypassed caller during page fault trap and returned back to 
great-caller instead.  That's why it did not execute background command in 
single quotes.

I discovered that HCORE tests missed a few bugs in instructions.  I was
told that HCORE tests show that all instructions passed tests but I ended
up a few serious bugs in a few instructions like CASEx, RET, and SKPC.
Also I discovered that HCORE was compiled for Rigel (KA670) because it
did not test a few floating instructions like EMODx, POLYx, and ACBx.
My KA630 firmware caught bugs in EMODF instruction so that I fixed it
by rewrote emulated EMODx instructions to pass KA630's tests.  HCORE
needs to being recompiled to test CVAX instructions instead of Rigel by 
modify in opt_compile.mar.

Well, I still struggled to get OpenVMS v7.2 booted.  It always results the
same when I tried to fix many bugs! :-(  It always stalls after
RQDX3 initialization and its unit scanned.  I tried OpenVMS v6.1 but
it resulted the same.

About KA630, I reviewed NetBSD/vax sources about TOY chip but noticed that
it checked valid bit in CSRD but my notes from Jeff did not mention that
and show unused bits instead.  Does anyone have a complete copy of
KA630's TOY chip tech manual?  That's why NetBSD always told me that TOY 
chip is not valid and check it regardless of TOY implementation with 
Jeff's notes.

Well, I now am implementing KA655 into my VAX emulator and was able to
run KA655 firmware.  However I discovered that my KA655 techincal manual
is not completed by comparing with Bob's simh emulator!  Does anyone
have a complete copy of CQBIC and SSC tech manuals to learn how does they
work internally?

Thank you!

-- Tim Stark

