The physical backplane was never replaced as I recall and I sat in the
machine room throughout most of the work.The backplane on the nearby
Dundee University KL-10  (a 1080 serial 1115) was replaced upgrading
it from a model A and a couple of other mods were done which would
have allowed it to run TOPS-20 but no backplane replacement was done
on the 2040. Other bits were replaced but not the backplane itself. 

>
>Don't get confused with all of those letters.  They were essentials
>order numbers for our paper mill.

Well there I agree you probably have me! I can only go by the letters
we saw externally. I vaguely recall that the difference between some
model B, C and E systems described to me by an engineer as "some are
essentially hardware identical but loaded with the older model
microcode because that's the model the customer ordered," However some
(probably most?)  weren't. Our 2040 presumably was.  As far as I know
all customer ship 1080s were model A as they first shipped *before*
model B development had been completed. It would not surprise me if
TOPS-10 development (including of course yourself) would have had a
model B or later 1080 though.

I do firmly recall that KL-10 serial #1115 and KL-10 #2172 (the 1080
and 2060 (ultimately) I was most familiar with had different physical
backplanes initially. KL-10 #2428 (Edinburgh University 1091 running
TOPS-10) was similar to the 2060 #2172, Part of the reason for
upgrading #1115 (apart from mysterious occasional faults) was to allow
F-S to only have to stock one spare backplane for all the Scottish
university KL-10s. 

At least two of the Scottish DEC-10/20 field service engineers still
work for Compaq and I see them occasionally if they're in doing any
work on our Alpha systems. Must ask if they have any souvenirs.

--
Alan


