In article <3C598091.9D77AC38@web.de>, Bernhard Mueck <Bernhard.Mueck@web.de> writes:

>Hi all,
>i have  a  new alpha box, my uname -a says
>
>OSF1  computer  V5.1 732 alpha
>
>When i start a program, it quits because it cannot allocate the memory,
>although i have enough physical ram.
>Is there something like in linux  /etc/security/limits where i can
>adjust the limits of stack data storage. ...

System wide process limits on virtual memory are kept in sysconfigtab
which can be maintained with the sysconfig(8) command.  The comments
at the beginning of the file have advice on maintaining it.  The
default per-process limits on virtual memory are typically 2 or 8
MB for the stack size with a maximum of 32 MB and 128 MB with a 
maximum of 1 GB for the data size.  Most shells have commands
for controlling the limits within the default and maximum size,
so you may not need to change the system limits.

The amount of page/swap space also limits the amount of virtual
memory available.  The tuning guide that is part of the documentation
set probably describes much of this.  If you don't have a printed
copy, HTML and PDF copies are on the documentation CDROM.

