Alan Greig <a.greig@virgin.net> writes:
> To the best of my recollection no  2040s were KL-10 model "A"s. KL-10

There are two different "model" concepts for KLs.  It's very unfortunate
terminology.

The PDP-10 System Reference Manual describes a "Model A" and a "Model B"
KL10.  These are sometimes referred to as the Model A and Model B
backplane, however, it is not the backplane itself that is different.
It is the complement of modules installed.  They are referring to the
*filled* backplane/cardcage.

Thus "Model A" refers to what is techncially known as a KL10-PA.  This
had 1280 words of microcode, did not support sections and had a 40 ns cycle
time.

"Model B" refers to the KL10-PV.  This had 2048 words of microcode, supported
sections, and had a 33 ns cycle time.

There was also a KL10-PW.  This doubled the size of the cache and pager,
but was generally similar to the KL10-PW.  From a software point of
view, it is still considered to be a "Model B", although there are in
fact a few minor software-visible differences (for which the
documentation was VERY difficult to find, since it was not in the System
Reference Manual).  Joe's page refers to it as "Model C"; that term may
have been in common usage but I've never seen that in any DEC
documentation.

There are also various types of the KL10 processor (complete set of
three boxes), KL10-A, -B, -C, -D, -E, and -R.  These do not map uniquely
to the -Px backplane model.  Joe's excellent model table has some minor
errors in this regard; for instance he lists both KL10-C and KL10-D as
being offered in both short and tall cabinets, which is not the case.

The KL10-A is only used in the 1080, was in tall cabinets, and has a
KL10-PA, though it could be field upgraded to a -PV or -PW.  It did not
have internal RH20 channels.

The KL10-B is used in the 1090, was in tall cabinets, and could use
either a KL10-PA or -PV

The KL10-C is used in the 2040 and 2050, was in short cabinets, and
could use either a KL10-PA or -PV.

The information I have at hand on the KL10-D is inconsistent.  I will
have to look at the microfiche to get the precise details.

The KL10-E and KL10-R were used in the 1091, 1095, 2060, and 2065, only
came in short cabinets, used floppy disks for boot media, and used
either the KL10-PV (1091, 2060) or KL10-PW (1095, 2065).

In summary, when someone says "Model A" or "Model B", it is ambiguous.
But it's most likely that they are referring to the KL10-PA or KL10-PV,
and not the system-level packaging.  A 2040 (KL10-C) was often configured
with a KL10-PA, in which case it can be considered to have a "Model A"
backplane.



