"Terry C. Shannon" <terryshannon@mediaone.net> writes:
> So, what do think about HP's proposed acquisition of Compaq in light of the
> European Union's decision not to oppose the deal?

The European Union only cares whether the result will form a monopoly.
They haven't said anything about whether the deal makes any business
sense; that's not their job.  Therefore their lack of opposition doesn't
offer any good reason to support the merger.

And the deal *still* does not make any business sense.  I find it quite
amusing that Walter Hewlett offers actual arguments as to why the merger
is bad, but the best that Carly Fiorina can offer by way of rebuttal is
personal attacks against Walter.  It doesn't matter what Walter's
background is, if he has good arguments against the merger.  It's not
like Carly's background in medieval history is any more relevant to
running HP.

Any time a big corporation has problems, the executives rush to start a
merger, acquisition, or divestiture.  It rarely achieves the promised
effect; often the cure is worse than the disease.  It's a wonder that
shareholders continue to put up with this style of so-called
"management" year after year.


