I need to boot a VAX-11/730 as a diskless satellite in a VAXcluster
   where most of the nodes run VMS5.5-2H4.  (Sometimes there's another
   system whose DEQNA prevents it from booting VMS5.5-2; that one is
   stuck at VMS5.2.)  This is a short-term/immediate requirement, rather
   than a spare-time hobby project, so I'm inclined to try to make do
   with the parts on hand rather than doing something ornate such as
   http://www.sparetimegizmos.com/Hardware/TU58_Emulator.htm did.
The relevant documents to which I have access are:
    -  EY-00014-DP "VAX/VMS Internals and Data Structures" (the edition
       that predates clustering and MOP booting);
    -  EK-SI730-IN-001 "VAX-11/730 Installation Guide";
    -  EK-1173-UG-003 (perhaps a typo for EK-11730-UG-003) "VAX-11/730
       Hardware User's Guide" pages iii through vi, 1-1 through 1-5, 2-1
       through 2-4, 2-39 through 2-49.
   I do not have a running system with a TU58 (so cannot write TU58s).
My first problem is that the TU58 capstan rollers have disintegrated or
   are disintegrating.  That makes TU58 operations even slower than
   usual, and necessitates many retries and much fiddling with the
   vertical position of the capstan roller on the motor shaft, to try to
   align the most solid area of a disintegrating capstan roller with the
   corresponding wheel in the cassette.  That mechanical problem aside,
   I believe that the only file which *must* come from TU58 is
   CONSOL.EXE, which implements the ">>>" prompt and corresponding
   commands.  Once the ">>>D"eposit command is available, the *.CPU
   files and VMB.EXE can be pushed in via the console serial line.  My
   first question: apart from ^C (which sometimes causes a failed TU58
   read to be retried), what can I enter to the "ROM>" prompt?  Is there
   any equivalent to the ">>>D"eposit command that would allow me to
   enter CONSOL.EXE via the console serial line if the TU58 fails to
   deliver it?  (A parenthetic question: where is CONSOL.EXE loaded?
   There appears to be no room for CONSOL.EXE in /C space--the *.CPU
   files occupy all of /C space.  /U space appears to be read-only.
   That leaves /P space.  If CONSOL.EXE goes into /P space, where?)
The TU58 %%%BOO.CMD files include comments on the ">>>L"oad commands
   which refer to "Version 57" and "Version 58".  Because some of the
   *.CPU files show minor changes between versions and other *.CPU files
   are identical between versions, I believe that the version numbers
   refer to the tape overall, rather than to the individual *.CPU files.
   I have no idea what version of VMS was current when those TU58s were
   published.  I have no reason to believe that the VMB.EXE that was
   then current, and that appears on the tapes, knows how to boot a
   system via MOP.  I therefore push in the VMB.EXE that travels with
   VMS5.5-2H4.  EY-00014-DP page 536 table 24-6 lists register inputs to
   VMB.EXE.  I believe, from inspecting TU58 %%%BOO.CMD files, that GR1
   should contain %x3 as TR number of adapter.  I set GR2 to %x3F948 to
   request booting via DEUNA or DELUA.  I set GR3 to 0, GR4 to 0, and
   GR5 to 0.  Second question: What should be the device type code in
   GR0<7:0>?  (Parenthetic question: where are the codes documented?)
   (My first thought was to simply try all possibilities, but I now
   suspect (because repeated trials with the same register starting
   values result in ?06 halts at different PCs) that VMB.EXE
   self-modifies; reloading VMB.EXE before each trial will be
   unpleasantly time consuming.)
I observe that the TU58 %%%BOO.CMD files that use the IDC explicitly
   specify the IDC's default vector %x00A8 in GR0<31:16> while
   %%%BOO.CMD files for other devices specify %x0000 (meaning "use the
   default") in GR0<31:16>.  Third question: is there any reason, in the
   DEUNA/DELUA case, to prefer specifying either %x0000 or %x0050 in
   GR0<31:16>?


