In article <a2l9s8$e2t$1@freenet9.carleton.ca>, du651@FreeNet.Carleton.CA (Joseph Ballantyne) writes:
:I need to boot a VAX-11/730 as a diskless satellite in a VAXcluster
:   where most of the nodes run VMS5.5-2H4.

  AFAIK, the code downloaded during a satellite bootstrap does not support 
  the VAX-11/730 platform.

  AFAIK, the VAX-11/730 has no way to solicit a download request, and the 
  VMB found on console media has no network capabilities.  VAXeln did have 
  a bootstrap that loaded an image containing MOP support, but (as mentioned 
  earlier) even if you got the MOP request transmitted, the secondary 
  bootstrap doesn't know about a VAX-11/730 box.

  It has been a long time, but my recollection is that the VAX-11/730 
  loads its brains from the TU58 before it gets rolling, and not just 
  the VMB.EXE (BOOT.EXE) and DEFBOO.CMD stuff -- a host of stuff (no
  pun intended) has to load before you get to DEFBOO and friends.

  There are ancient discussions around the correct ordering of the
  contents of the console TU58 around -- alphanumeric order makes the
  normally slow TU58 access required during the bootstrap even worse.

  I used a cut-down version of OpenVMS VAX stuffed onto an RL02 for service 
  and maintenance purposes -- I used to stuff enough of (as it was then 
  known) VAX/VMS V4.x and DECnet for a minimal bootstrap and BACKUP over 
  DECnet into about 3MB of disk.  This is the approach I would take here,
  if you have an RL02 around -- though you did say the box was diskless, 
  sometimes folks (quite reasonably :-) forget about this disk drive.

  The only way I know to get this box to bootstrap while completely diskless 
  would involve writing a new secondary (or tertiary) loader image for it.
  And I am not certain that a VAX-11/730 system with one or two failed TU58 
  console drives would function.  (The VAX-11/730 boxes I've seen have all 
  had two TU58 drives.  I'll assume you've checked the alternate TU58 drive 
  -- on some VAX-11/730 configurations, you had to pull the processor out 
  on the sliders to gain access to the side-mounted TU58 drive.)

