Hoff Hoffman wrote:
>AFAIK, the code downloaded during a satellite bootstrap does not
>  support the VAX-11/730 platform.
I'm now prepared to be disappointed.  I had observed that the VAX-11/730
   is enough of a VAX to run VMB, and enough of a VAX to run VMS, and
   naively failed to consider that one of the intermediate steps between
   VMB and VMS might choke on it.
>AFAIK, the VAX-11/730 has no way to solicit a download request, and the
>  VMB found on console media has no network capabilities.
That was my working assumption, and that was why I ignored the console
   tape VMB and used the VMS5.5-2H4 VMB instead.  I note that the
   VMS5.5-2H4 VMB is more than twice as big as the VAX-11/730 console
   VMB, and I assume that the added bulk results in part from the
   addition of the ability to be a MOP client.
>...VAX-11/730 loads its brains from the TU58 before it gets rolling...
Exactly.  The *.CPU files (of which it loads 8 or 9, depending on
   hardware options) teach it how to emulate a VAX.
>...correct ordering of the contents of the console TU58...
Apparently it was close to standard practice for VAX-11/730 users to
   create stripped-down TU58 console tapes which contained exactly the
   files required for their hardware configuration, in exactly the order
   they were used during boot.  I know I did it.  :)
>...approach I would take here, if you have an RL02 around...
There *is* an RL02.  It's possible that it works.  Once (*if*) I get VMS
   running (via the console ">>>D"eposit route), I'll be in a position
   to put a minimal system on a RL02 pack.  Of course, once I get VMS
   running, I won't *need* to put a minimal system on a RL02 pack.  At
   the moment, same as I don't have a way to write a TU58, I don't have
   a way to write a RL02.
>The only way I know to get this box to bootstrap while completely
>  diskless would involve writing a new secondary (or tertiary) loader
>  image for it.
Thanks for the warning about this potential/probable obstruction.
>...not certain that a VAX-11/730...with...failed TU58...drives would
>  function.
I'm optimistic on this point.  VMS doesn't need anything from the
   console TU58 in normal operation, after it's booted.  I had a habit,
   in situations where unattended booting was not required, of pulling
   the TU58 tape partly out of the side TU58 drive, to delay
   deterioration of that TU58's capstan roller.  (If the TU58 console
   tape was left physically mounted, the hard puck in the cassette
   created a dent in the drive's soft capstan roller.)  VMS thus ran
   without access to the TU58 console tape.

James Lothian wrote:
>A slice off...automotive hose will make a good replacement roller.
Brilliant!  I'd been fishing around mentally for possible substitution
   methods, but I hadn't thought of that (or any other useful
   possibility).  I need new lateral-thinking circuits.


