>Without any spare parts, it is difficult to repair a broken-down computer.
>AFAIK, Compaq sold off all its spare parts inventory for DEC Wintel computers (I
>have some of that) and most early Alphas.  Now, if one decides to have Compaq
>take ones money in return for repair of an older DEC box, be prepared for a long
>wait as Compaq calls all of its old DEC dealers to scour the globe for spare
>parts.
>
>Over a year ago, I gave up on some of the older DEC Wintel hardware, stripped
>the computers, scrapped the chasses, kept boards and assorted widgets.  There is
>only so much warehouse space.  Not too long thereafter, I got a call from
>another dealer looking for the parts I'd recently scrapped, to sell back to
>Compaq... Ben Myers
>

There seems to be a belief in the short lifespan of systems in the Unix and
Windows community. With Windows this sort of makes sense -  the latest versions
of bloated software require more powerful systems. However if you bought a
system sometime ago and sized it correctly at that time and have not
experienced massive growth in the number of users since then why replace it.

In the case of the 2100 if you do need improved performance then various
options may be possible depending on the configuration you bought.
This is a multi-cpu system hence if you bought a system
with a single cpu you can add in 3 more cpus. The 2100 also came with various
generations of CPUs hence you may also be able to increase cpu performance by
replacing older cpus with slightly later cpus.

As to getting spare parts. The 2100 was DECs most succesful system at the time.
There are tons of spare parts available.

Last year I increased the number of CPUs on each of my two 2100 5/300 systems
to 3 on each and increased the memory from 512MB to 1.25Gb. This was a lot
cheaper than buying new systems.

(And yes these systems are and have always been on maintenance contract).

It used to be that DEC was proud to say that the latest version of VMS would 
run on practically every VMS capable VAX or Alpha system they every produced
going right back to the VAX 11/780.
Unfortunately even its short life we have already seen that Tru64 has dropped
support for a fair number of systems.


