All those and more! In fact, a veritable panoply of DECpress existed at one
time or another...

In the beginning, back in the dark old days of the late 70s there was RSTS
Professional, which in 1981 begat DEC Professional and then itself morphed
into VAX Professional in 1982. Personal and Professional came and went from
one end of the Rainbow to the other in 83-84. That was in Spring House, PA.
Charlie Matco made his debut under the Terry Shannon pseudonym in early
1983, in 1984 Matco relocated from Roswell, NM, where he was dwelling among
the Space Aliens, to Spring House, PA. Spring House is close to Ambler and
Fort Washington, if anyone cares.

While Matco was making his First of Many Pilgrimages, Hardcopy erupted on
the Left Coast. And in Taxachusetts, Digital Review: The Magazine was born.
The first sighting of a Charles Matco article in Digital Review was October
1985. Terry Shannon hisself arrived in Beantown the same month. Coincidence?
You decide.

In 1986, Digital Review: The Magazine morphed into Digital Review: the
Weekly Magaloid. This was widely regarded as a pre-emptive move by Ziff
Davis against rival IDG, who was spinning up Digital News, a twice-monthly
newspaper/magazine or Magaloid.

In early 1988, the Real Charlie Matco left the Digital Review building. A
faux Matco lived on.

Sometime thereafter, as cracks appeared in the vaunted Digital armor,
Digital Review and Digital News were acquired by Cahners Publishing and
merged into Digital News and Review. This would have been 1992 or 1993. The
real Matco returned to the pages of DN&R for a brief spell, writing under
his own named as well as his Terry Shannon pseudonym.

Hardcopy went teats-up. So did the little-known "Computers r Digital," a
magazine published in Red Bank, NJ.

The original owners of DEC Professional and VAX Professional already had
bailed out and made a pretty penny selling off their literary assets to
Cardinal Business Media, who struggled on for a few years before folding.

Digital News and Review folded in the mid-90s and was partially reincarnated
as BackOffice Magazine. The reincarnation did not last for long, perhaps due
in no small part to a ghastly Philadelphia Experiment.

The foreign DEC trade press, which included ON$DEC, DEC Computing, DEC User,
and several others, also came and went.

So the last Dog barking is SKC, which is now in its ninth year. If memory
serves me correctly (and we sure as hell didn't have ECC back in them days)
this will be my twentieth year covering DECpaq in the pages of one
publication or another.

I hope SKC continues on, perhaps under yet another new name (SKC being the
third instantiation of the Newsletter That Takes No Prisoners) because at
age 50 I am getting too old to get a Real Job anyplace.









