On 17 Jan 2002, glen herrmannsfeldt wrote:
> >>   1) PDP-10 is the only architecture I've worked with which has no user
> >>   accessable CPU registers.  One tends to use the accumulators on
> >>   a PDP-10 as one would use registers on other systems, so for this
> >>   comment I'll use the generic word register for both:  the PDP-10 is
> >>   the only system I've used on which one register (accumulator 0) is
> >>   sometimes a real register and sometimes ignored.  Tripped up over
> >>   that one many times.
> I think more do than don't.  S/360 (S/370, S/390), as a CISC architecture,
> and most RISC architectures.  There often needs to be an instruction
> form that doesn't do something, but that one shouldn't waste a whole
> bit on.

What he's complaining about is that accumulator 0 can't be used as an
index register, because 0 in the index register field means that there
isn't any indexing.  So you can only have 15 index registers instead of
all 16 accumulators.  Aww...

> >>
> >>   2) The use of a conditional skip instead of a conditional branch.
> >>   Assembly language programmers just don't think that way.
> >	TRNE REALITY
> >	JUMPE AC,NONSENSE	; because using both is better?
> It can only work with fixed size instructions.  Skip over an unconditional
> branch is as easy for a compiler to generate.  (Some architectures
> have short conditional branch and long unconditional branch).

Fortunately, the PDP-10 did not have branches.  You could do branching if
you really wanted to, but very few people really wanted to unless they
were writing self-relocating code ala S/360.

The PDP-10 had jumps instead, the difference being that the target of the
jump is an address rather than an offset from current instruction as in a
branch.

Also, there certainly *were* conditional jumps.  There were 8 based upon
the contents of an accumulator vs zero.  There was also a very useful
conditional jump that was effectively the Fortran DO loop instruction.


> >>   3) The difficulties one had to go through to manipulate characters
> >>   in 36 bits words as 7 bit bytes, and the impossibility of dumping 7
> >>   bit characters in a recognizeable representation in a hex, octal, or
> >>   any other commonly used radix.
> >	ILDB AC,CHRSTRN
> >	IDPB AC,HEXSTRN	;because he likes hex?
> >	JUMPNE AC,.-2		;because we can!
> Yes, but it is inconvenient.  At the time, the 8 bit byte wasn't as
> popular and ASCII was a seven bit code.

It is, however, extremely convenient to access any size bit field without
having to do masks and shifts.

> >>   4) The limitted address range, even extended addressing was limitted to
> >>   23 bits.
> This is a problem.  Somewhere in "Hennessy & Patterson" it states
> that the one mistake that can't be overcome is too small an address
> space.

In 1964, 18 bits was a huge address space.  Even today, it is good enough
for many applications because 18 bits on a PDP-10 was the equivalent of
20-21 bits on a byte-oriented machine.  Similarly, the 23-bit mode on the
KLs translates into 25-26 bits, so unless your application is bigger than
32MB it isn't a significant limit.  And that was a limit on that CPU, not
on the architecture.  Note that some purportedly 32-bit CPUs had lesser
(e.g. 24-bit) address limits.

The architecture actually defines 30-bit addressing, which is at least
equivalent to any 32-bit byte-oriented processor.

Yes, it's inferior to the 64-bit machines out there, but 32-bits hasn't
exactly died yet.

> >>   5) The requirement (except on the 2020) to use a PDP-11 in order to
> >>   communicate with the outside world.  Typically an 11/40 or 11/60 serial
> >>   and print front end and an 11/34A DECnet front end.
> >	DATAO DEV,WHATEVER	;Hey, ImNoCray!
> This isn't an architecture constraint, so I don't count it.

As well you shouldn't; it's just nonsense.  The idea of using a front end
CPU was quite politically correct at the time the KL10 processor was
designed, but there were processors before and after that time which did
not use front ends.

> >>   6) The requirement for every fork to initialize it's own stack.   And
> >>   the ability of some instructions to use any accumulator as the stack
> >>   pointer, while other instruction assumed the standard stack pointer.
> >>   So when is the stack pointer in the stack pointer?
> >	AOS (SP)
> >	POPJ SP,	;because he hates skip returns!
> The decision over where stacks get created is one reason for not
> liking stack based calling conventions.

Well, his complaint here is also nonsense.  There was no machine
instruction that assuming any register as a stack pointer, although it was
certainly the convention to use register 17 for that purpose.  A single
pseudo instruction in *one* of the operating systems, used for system call
error handling, assumed register 17, as did a macro library on that
operating system.

> >>   7) Not enough accumulators.
> Not so bad considering when it was designed.  It is enough for
> good register allocation algorithms to work with.

16 accumulators is certainly good enough for most assembly language
applications.  Compiler writers generally are the guys who want more.

> >>   8) No support for paging (virtual memory concepts were limitted to
> >>   swapping).
> I don't know if this is a fundamental problem.

His complaint here was total nonsense.  Demand paging was invented on the
PDP-10.  In fact, what makes the PDP-10 so hard to emulate is getting the
pager right, because it was so extremely sophisticated.  Paging stuff that
is generally done in OS software today was done in hardware or microcode
on the PDP-10.

-- Mark --

