> In the beginning, there were small computers, and they did not have much
> memory.  Yet the hackers said, "let there be a filesystem" and behold!
> there was a filesystem.  But there was not much space, and the computers
> used word sizes that were multiples of 6 (12, 18, and 36), so the names of
> the files where stored, even unto the program names, in a six-bit form of
> ASCII which started with 000 as space and ended with 077 as underscore.
> 
> And the filesystem was exceedingly good and admired throughout the land,
> even though it had no lower case, since at that time most terminals were
> uppercase only devices, yea, even the VT05s which were displays and could
> run the good version of TECO.
> 
> Then one day, full ASCII terminals did appear, and there was great
> rejoicing; for the users could enter and display text in a form which did
> appear good to them.  But soon a darkness fell upon the land, for none of
> the programs did recognize the lower case, nay, not even unto the commands
> and the file names.  And the users did gather, and formed a lynch mob, and
> threatened the hackers with a purchase of IBM equipment if this were not
> fixed.
> 
> And the hackers were troubled, since fixing the filesystem was not
> practical, and the righteous anger of the users was not swayed by the
> response of "use the CAPS LOCK key."  So the hackers did come up with an
> idea, which was to treat lowercase alphabetics as uppercase alphabetics
> for the purpose of commands and filenames, since the other characters from
> 0140 to 0176 were specials and could be disregarded.
> 
> And the idea worked, and there was once again great rejoicing in the land.
> 
> And then the developers of Tenex did come up with a filesystem which used
> ASCII instead of SIXBIT, but they had few applications, and had to be
> compatible with the older operating system.  So although their filesystem
> was case-dependent, they decreed that the system calls should all coerce
> lowercase letters to upper case, so that compatibility may be achieved,
> and no new lynch mob shall form.
> 
> And so it was done, and TOPS-20 inherited this behavior from Tenex.
> TOPS-20 has a case-dependent filesystem, but you have to work to get
> lowercase letters or special characters (such as "*") into filenames by
> quoting each such character with CTRL/V.
> 
> But it worked, and there was rejoicing throughout the land.
> 
> Then the developers of UNIX built their filesystem, and it was ASCII, and
> they had full ASCII terminals, and they had no need to be compatible with
> 1960s operating systems.  And it worked, and there was rejoicing
> throughout the land.
> 
