"Fabio Cardoso" <fabiopenvms@yahoo.com.br> wrote in message
news:20020115113240.60185.qmail@web20206.mail.yahoo.com...
It is time to create a new independent magazine
for OpenVMS. Should be sponsored by Encompass, with
the OpenVMS software companies making ads, and
with interview with users (companies) and
sysadmins. should be launched each 3 or 6 months
per year.

Having been in this business for a while (like for about 18 years now) I'd
like to make a few observations:

Magazines are costly to start up and generally generate negative cash flow
for their first several years.

Magazines require an editorial staff and an advertising staff.

Magazines are either "controlled circulation" (free to qualified subscribers
who fit a profile) or subscription based. Controlled-circ magazines are 100
percent dependent on advertising revenue. Some subscription-based magazines
are 100 percent subscriber-funded (eg my newsletter, which does not take
advertising), others rely on a mix of ads and subscriber revenue.

Recall that in the mid-80s there were half a dozen DEC-specific magazines in
the USA and another half-dozen worldwide. All of them folded due to lack of
advertising revenue (industry standards reared their ugly heads).

The proposal you mention (Encompass sponsorship, ads from VMS companies,
etc) might work but would require a hell of a lot of work and up-front
revenue which Encompass does not have.

That said, Encompass US Inc.'s sister organization ITUG has a monthly
magazine called the Connection. It comes as part of the $150 ITUG annual
membership fee. It is profitable because it is heavily subsidized by
NSK-centric vendors who realize that the Connection is the single best place
to get the message out to their target audience.

DECUS US's track record with magazines is not good. In the 1980s we had free
SIG newsletters which were paid for by Digital (who of course funded DECUS
heavily) and written and produced by the various SIGs. In the late 80s a
decision was made to create a combined SIGS Newsletter on a
paid-subscription basis. This failed miserably.

DECUS had a glossy quarterly magazine for about a year, it failed too.

And the moral is... magazines are easier said than done. Heck, I'd love to
be the publisher/managing editor of a VMS journal, but I just don't see it
happening. And I can assure you that I don't have the requisite $500K-$1M
capital needed for a successful launch!

cheers,

terry s




