In article <u49g4f506k1826@corp.supernews.com>, "NeoSpawn" <mb_death(at)hotmail.com> writes:

  Please pick a title relevent to the question -- this will be most
  likely to draw the attention of folks that know the answer to your
  question -- getting you an answer in the least time.  Thanks!

:My DEC 3000/300LX has two SCSI hard drives...
:...I want them to work and I have no choice but to run headless 
:because I don't have any console equipment for a DEC system.

  Please see the OpenVMS FAQ for details on running a system via 
  the serial console.  On the DEC 3000 series, the system should
  select the alternate console when it cannot locate the workstation
  keyboard and mouse (again, see the FAQ for details on compatibility)
  or (and I believe it is "or" and not "and") when the Motherboard W2 
  hardware jumper (alternate console select) is set to pins 2-3 to 
  select the serial console.  W2 is located along the edge of the 
  motherboard near the SIMM array, and specifically on the motherboard 
  edge toward the fan.  The typical baud rate and such is listed in the 
  FAQ -- 9600 8N1, IIRC.

:I'm not dumb enough to even ask about Windows NT.

  Your relative intelligence as a questioner is not relevent here, as
  Microsoft Windows NT was never supported on TURBOchannel systems, and
  you have one of these TURBOchannel boxes. 

  The DEC 3000 model 300L lacks TURBOchannel; there are a handful of 
  DEC 3000 model 300-series variants around.  (And none of them have 
  Microsoft Windows support.)

: I have yet to find a Linux that will work, I don't know if I want to try 
:NetBSD, I can't seem to find any other decent Unix that I can download, 
:and I really REALLY don't want to try net-booting the thing.  

  Please see the OpenVMS hobbyist program, referenced in the OpenVMS
  FAQ.  If you don't want to run OpenVMS on this box, I'd encourage
  you to swap it with a hobbyist that does want to.

  If you are interested running a non-current version of Tru64 UNIX, a 
  hobbyist program is also available for that operating system.  AFAIK, 
  current versions of Tru64 UNIX do not have support for TURBOchannel 
  systems such as the DEC 3000 series, and I do not particularly know 
  which was the last Tru64 UNIX version with support for this box.

:Unfortunately it doesn't have a floppy and I read that normal SCSI CD-ROMs 
:won't work right with them.

  Please see the OpenVMS FAQ for an introduction to the 512-byte block
  CD-ROM expectations.

: Oh, what must I do to get this thing running?  Will I have to configure 
:a netboot server after all, or am I better off selling it to a DEC wizard 
:(like all you people here! :)  I'd love to play on it a bit, but 
:unfortunately my options seem terribly limited.

  Your options depend highly on what you want to do -- but TURBOchannel 
  I/O widgets are comparatively rare, as hardware goes.

  Console (SRM) firmware information is referenced in the OpenVMS FAQ.

