i would recommend you find out what hard drives are installed.

if they are RZ28 or RZ28Bs, then a 3 inch test can sometimes resurrect
them - i have run into several that a 3 inch drop has "unsticktified"
enough so that they have spun up properly after that.  

the 3000/300 is a nice machine in terms of its decstation 5000/2X
form factor, but it's extremely old/slow nowadays, in particular with
half the memory bandwidth of the 400/500 series turbochannel machines.

if you are destroying content/moving things around you might also want
to renumber and go with DKA0 and DKA100 rather than what you've been
stuck with - you can also replace the drives with any 50pin scsi device
though they weren't rated for internal 7200rpm drives.

they usually did have onboard video (HX graphics - 8 bit 1280x1024)
and could be used as a headful workstation if you can find the appropriate
KB, mouse and the cable to connect them to the base, and of course the
appropriate monitor cable.  

they should run upto Tru64 5.0 and will certainly run earlier variants
such as DEC Unix 4.0X (where X is A through to G) or even OSF/1 3.2

cheers,

-jason


