Hi,

     I had a similar experience with the power supply for a BA123 
chassis.  It was set up correctly, but smoked excessively when powered 
up, along with some loud buzzing and some sparks shooting out of the top 
of the power supply.  Upon opening the supply, I see that one of the big 
power transistors smoked, and doubt that it was the cause of the 
problem, but it did definitely emit sparks!  I'm not sure if the power 
supply blew up because of not enough load (no hard drives installed in 
the chassis, just the CPU and some other random boards (no RAM).

     I found an interesting article on the web about repairing switching 
supplies (deals with PC power supplies) but the theory should be the 
same.  Here's the link:

www.nutsvolts.com/PDF_Files/PSRepair.pdf

     I believe that in my case, the switching circuit which modulates 
the 300V high-voltage side went bad.  The thing that I don't know is 
what other "cascading damage" occurred as a result.  Two questions: has 
anyone tried to repair a DEC power supply similar to this (and with what 
success?) and (2) if one power transistor fails, will all the other ones 
blow out as well?

     I measured the resistance of the outputs, and found that they are 
all between 100 and 200 ohms.  From what I read in the above article, it 
seems likely that the low-voltage regulator portion of the supply is ok.

     Howard



