They were successful enough that IBM provided first an emulator product (added
hardware) for the 360, and later a simulator (software) product for the 370; at
the University of Chicago, many parts of the accounting system were still 1401
code from the early 1960s until autumn 1982, when my office mate finished her
project of documenting the uncommented Autocoder listings. [1]

Besides, they were successful in another way:  I first learned to program on a
1401 with FORTRAN IV.  If not for that, I'd not be where I am today, and you'd
not have the opportunity to read this screed.

[1] She had made the mistake, in early autumn 1979, of going to the assistant
manager of the Financial Systems group and saying, "I don't have enough to do."
I'm fairly sure she erased that phrase from her vocabulary.

