On the CNN Money site (formerly CNNFN) http://money.cnn.com appears
the following headline "Signs of  Life for PCs". Clicking the link
leads to the report on Compaq's Q4 figures. Actually reading the CNN
report makes it clear that PCs were dragging the company down but
whoever writes the headlines obviously hadn't read the article. After
all everyone knows Compaq is a PC company so if it produces figures
ahead of expectations then obviously PCs must be showing signs of
life. Compaq fail to project their correct image yet again. 

I note also that the largest Q3-Q4 revenue increase came from products
which Compaq are killing: Tru64 on Alphaservers. Although, again, I
believe a huge US military sale accounts for the bulk of this.
Assuming that the merger fails and Compaq still license HP-UX as is
suggested who would you buy an IA64 based HP-UX system from: Compaq or
HP if you were the US military? Answer quite possibly neither I
suspect.

Here's a couple of quotes from the CNN report making it clear there is
no "signs of life for PCs" yet that's what the blithering idiot at CNN
who wrote the headline deduced.

"Meanwhile, the company's PC business weighed on the bottom line,
posting an operating loss for the quarter."

and

"In Compaq's enterprise computing segment - which includes servers and
data-storage systems - revenue totaled $2.7 billion, and that unit
posted operating income of $56 million.

The weak link in Compaq's business during the fourth quarter was its
PC business, which logged revenue of $3.8 billion and an operating
loss of $69 million.

Compaq has been struggling in the PC market amid dwindling demand and
increased pricing pressure. The company has taken a number of steps
over the past year to improve profits there, including laying off
workers, outsourcing the production of its portable computers and
working off inventories.

While the PC unit's losses have been narrowing, Compaq executives said
they still have a way to go and they do not expect it to return to
profitability until the second half of this year." 



