> Their primary job is to get one's attention, not to supply accurate
insight.

The primary job of the trade press is to make money. They do this by
providing content that serves as a framework for advertisements. This holds
true for "controlled circulation" (free to qualified subscribers, eg
ComputerWorld et al) and paid-subscription (eg Business Week, etc)
publications alike.
>
> Apologies in advance to Terry, whose accurate, insightful, and intelligent
> writings are not included in this trashing of the 'press'.
>

It is arguable that the quality of IT trade press publications has declined
significantly in the past decade. The content of circa-1980's DEC-specific
publications, notably Digital Review in the US and DEC User in the UK,
reflected substantial technical expertise. The move to "industry standard
computing" did away with the vendor-specific publications and with the
technical focus of the writers and reporters.

This, of course, provides me with a nice little niche, but it's sad to see
the paucity of technical content and insight in the mainstream media.




