> Bob Koehler wrote:
> >    Back to reality.  A great bit of VMS has since been rewritten in
> >    BLISS or other HLL.  I know of VAX Macro code fomr VMS 2.x that
> >    became BLISS by VMS 4.0.
> 
> 
> When writing kernel code or deep down stuff for the OS, does using a compiler
> with lots of optimization for the harware ever cause serious problems ?  (I am
> thinking of instruction ordering, and variable "elimination" by keeping them
> in registers, and possible many other factors).

In BLISS you can declare a variable to be VOLATILE so it isn't kept in
a register and, IIRC, is always fetched from its memory address for
each use.

I wrote power-on boot code for a controller board based on an embedded
MIPS processor mostly in BLISS and as far as I can remember (this is
going back about 10 years) it needed almost no assembly code.  I can
also remember having a hell of an argument about using BLISS rather
than C.  I won when I showed the BLISS code used far less ROM space
than C.

jb (ex-DEC, who's a lawyer these days)


