JF Mezei <jfmezei.spamnot@videotron.ca> wrote in message news:<3C3A146D.9BF412F9@videotron.ca>...
: When writing kernel code or deep down stuff for the OS, does using a 
: compiler with lots of optimization for the harware ever cause serious 
: problems ?  

  Ever?  Sure.  But that is not particularly meaningful answer, of course.

  I've debugged bugs that were latent, and that that were uncovered by the 
  optimizer -- in entirely user-mode code.  The failures do tend to be more 
  dramatic within kernel-mode code of course, but the potential for problems 
  is not specific to the operating system nor to privileged-mode code.  
  (Most any optimizer tends to be a useful tool for uncovering latent code 
  bugs, after all.  More than a few programmers have blamed the compiler
  or the optimizer for these latent bugs, too -- the first time I coded
  one of these bugs, I blamed the optimizer.  This is not to say that the 
  optimizer can't ever introduce bugs, I've seen that happen too.)

: (I am thinking of instruction ordering, and variable "elimination" by 
: keeping them in registers, and possible many other factors).

  The Alpha real-time or shared-memory programmer must be familiar with the 
  sequence points and with the trap and memory constructs barriers -- not 
  only the compiler, but the microprocessor can also re-order and can also 
  cache stuff.  This is nothing particularly new -- various VAX boxes have 
  similar considerations.

  For an introduction into some of the usual sorts of programming mistakes
  and considerations, please review topics (1661), (2681), (4647) and (6099) 
  over in the Ask The Wizard area.  These are an introduction, of course.

  There are language keywords around that will keep a variable in memory 
  (and not in a register; eg: "volatile") -- volatile-like keywords are 
  particularly useful for I/O space references.  (You must be careful to 
  have the correct widget marked as volatile, too.  You probably don't 
  want to end up with a memory-based pointer to an object stored in a 
  register, of course.  :-)  Volatile doesn't help with memory caching
  and memory access resequencing, however...

  There is a list of the hardware instructions that are available in I/O 
  space listed in the documentation set -- related information in the 
  manuals can provide details, as can the usual discussions around shared
  memory and synchronization techniques.  The circa-1982 VAX hardware 
  manual had such a list, and the second edition of the VAX Architecture
  Manual (1987, 1991) has I/O space reference details on pages 321 and 322.

  As a start, please read and heed the OpenVMS Programming Concepts manual.

