<jmfbahciv@aol.com> wrote in message news:a1pajb$pdl$3@bob.news.rcn.net...

...

> The point of a filename is to give the operating system
> a method of distingishing its contents from another file.

Not in any system I've ever worked with/on:  they've all had 'file
identifiers' (typically binary offsets into some kind of 'inode'-ish
array/'Index' file) for that purpose (though admittedly MS-DOS/Win9x
effectively just have a start-block address).

And several of these systems have had mechanisms that allowed a single file
to be addressed by multiple names ('hard', as distinct from 'symbolic',
links).

> The purpose of a filename is NOT to document the contents
> exhaustively.

No, but it is (usually) meant to be significant to humans in at least
superficially documenting the contents.

  Thus a filename is chosen in terms of the
> computing biz, not the spoken language.

On any system intended to be used by non-programmers (the only kind of file
system I'm particularly interested in these days) that is not true.

  I do not want
> to have to take a glance at a list of files on the
> system and have to make sure that I'm using the exact
> font as the writer of the data used.  If I have to worry
> about that, then the font had better be an attribute stored
> with other file information.

On that we agree.

- bill





