>Not in any system I've ever worked with/on:  they've all had 'file
>identifiers' (typically binary offsets into some kind of 'inode'-ish
>array/'Index' file) for that purpose (though admittedly MS-DOS/Win9x
>effectively just have a start-block address).

No, no.  [frustrated emoticon trying to think how to describe this].
All right, let's try this, Bill.  Think about transferring a disk
file to a magtape.  The filename is the method of distinguishing
which bits should be collectively associated.  The way we "wrote"
files to magtape during a BACKUP was really one great big file.
However, the restore of that magtape used the filenames to
move the collections of bits back to the disk.  If I understand 
Unix' implementation, their inode is an internal mechanism
to arrange the data on a disk.  TOPS-10 has a similar mechanism
but it's only visible to the FILFND piece of the monitor. 
At least, I think that's piece.  The point is that this
level of the detail is only known by the monitor and not
by any user mode program.  It's a way to keep application
programmers from meddling with the real bit arrangements.

>
>And several of these systems have had mechanisms 
>that allowed a single file
>to be addressed by multiple names ('hard', as distinct from 'symbolic',
>links).

Sure.  You can do the same on TOPS-10.  But we didn't let any old
user or programmer do it.  In some cases, you had to have the
system stand-alone to mess around with stuff like that.

>
>> The purpose of a filename is NOT to document the contents
>> exhaustively.
>
>No, but it is (usually) meant to be significant to humans in at least
>superficially documenting the contents.

Sure that's a feature that we used a lot.  Mostly it was used
to distinguish the "group" the file belonged to.  If the filename
and/or extension had the letters "FOR" in them, I was pretty
sure that I had to go talk to the FORTRAN group about it.  
But this convenience was due to an internal naming standard 
that we imposed on ourselves.  We also reserved certain 
character arrangements for DEC-only use.  That way, there
was a good chance that we wouldn't break customer software.


>
>  Thus a filename is chosen in terms of the
>> computing biz, not the spoken language.
>
>On any system intended to be used by non-programmers 
>(the only kind of file
>system I'm particularly interested in these days) that is not true.

An English sentence had a subject and a predicate.  Are you telling
me that a filename has to include both when the users are
not programmers?  I don't believe that.  I worked with a lot
of non-programmers.  They had no problems thinking about files
in terms of the OS definitions.  
>
>  I do not want
>> to have to take a glance at a list of files on the
>> system and have to make sure that I'm using the exact
>> font as the writer of the data used.  If I have to worry
>> about that, then the font had better be an attribute stored
>> with other file information.
>
>On that we agree.

This is my main point.  If there are going to be variations
in the character interpretations, there damn well better be
an attribute saying how each file was created so that
a program OPENing the file, given a user/program specification,
can make some intelligent decisions when errors or redudancy
occurs.

/BAH

