There are other exceptions. Heavenly bodies. (Sun, Moon, Earth versus sun
[=some other star, not ours], moon [=to bare the buttocks], earth
[=electrical term]). I (always capitalised, you never say 'i went to the
shops' (unless you are in a hurry/lazy ;).
Sometimes, when used in a descriptive sense. U-bend. S-bend and Z-bend [from
roads],
rarely s-bend or z-bend. 'Hammer' as in a person, as opposed to 'hammer' (a
tool).
'Swede' [=a person from Sweden] and 'swede' [=a variety of turnip].
'N-complete' [from mathematics] more often than 'n-complete'. 'Death' (the
guy with the scythe) versus 'death' (the more normal meaning) (and the same
for War, Pestilence and Famine). 'the Bible' [=the book in general] as
opposed to 'the bible' [=as in referring to a specific bible].
'North','South', 'East' and 'West' (although capitals on the cardinal points
of the compass are becoming rare and falling out of use). 'the Government'
[=our own Government] as opposed to 'the government' [=the act of
governing].

Most of these are probably specific to British English, but that's no reason
to just ignore them. Jeez, why, when I select British English on my PC, does
it still talk about desktop 'colors', and says 'Connecting <name-of-my-ISP>'
as opposed to the correct 'Connecting to <name-of-my-isp>'. And why is my
choice of measurement system limited to 'Metric' and 'U.S.'. Where's
'Imperial'? And 'Favorites', not 'Favourites'. At least, all of this is
correct from my point of view, and since I asked for British
English, why am I not getting it??? Oh and of course, it should have a
'Defenestrate'
option instead of 'Uninstall Windows' ;-)

Returning to the topic though, I think a file system should be case
insensitive, but
allow names to be stored in mixed-case, as long as it was case-insensitively
unique (no Foo.bar and FOO.BAR, for example). For foreign languages, it
should use the rules relevant to that language. If characters appear
identical between languages, or have the same alphabetical function (such as
� and ss in German), it should treat these as equivalents. This does get to
start a bit of a problem if you have, say, �nitions and munitions. The
answer is that the file system should use fuzzy matching UNLESS you have a
case such as this where the two files are clearly different. It should also
warn the application about this ambiguity. The application should then be
nice enough to tell you...

A non-Western alphabet like Arabic, Korean, Japanese or Tamil, should use
the standard transliteration mechanisim accepted for these languages. If one
exists, that is! The OS's utilities should be able to display the file name
in its original character set, plus it should be transliterated into your
own local character set. So,
you could send a Russian a document written by a friend in Korea, and all of
you would
be able to use _different_ filenames to refer to the _same_ file. The file
name might not make any sense to you, but that's a semantics problem. You
could have the computer make an attempt to translate the name, but that's a
problem for the software, not the OS [Not yet anyway - but maybe one day !].

Chinese might be a problem, since very many characters transliterate to the
same letters.. Probably a workaround would be necessary, such as instead of
the file 'yiyiyiyi' it was 'yi104yi28yi304yi47' where the number refers to
the order of its appearance in the character set. My father's encyclopedias
(Collier, 1969) have an
interesting illustration of this problem in the entry for 'CHINESE' called
the story of
Aunt Yi's Pancreas, consisting of about 20-odd characters, all of which are
pronounced 'yi' in one of four ways...

This is probably the best solution. It gives you filenames that are ugly,
but usable _whatever your local language_. Details such as the language of
the filename and its local transliteration should be stored as file
metadata. If you move a file from, say, a Cyrillic to an Arabic machine, the
Arabic machine should check the local language set in the file metadata and,
re-tag it as Arabic and re-transliterate it from the original filename using
the rules appropriate for Arabic. This should be transparent to the user. A
user should optionally be allowed to place their own local name in the
metadata. So if I received a file from a Korean about bread imports, I could
give it the English name 'Bread Imports' as a secondary name... but, this is
the neat thing, it would still retain its Korean name, and when I e-mailed
it back to him, he would still see the Korean name. Ideally the metadata
should be able to store many such local names. These could even be used as
part of the OS security; files that are hidden UNLESS you are a member of a
group that has access to them (and then it shows you the file name, in
whatever local language you are using), all governed by the file metadata.

Wonder if anyone's working on this sort of thing? Did Plan 9 implement
anything like this?

Or have I just given the filesystem guys a great idea on a silver platter
for free? Darn! ;-)

-Malcolm.

