I know the people who wrote that sofware. They had full control of
the tools used to look up words in it, and specifically included both
cases because the case IS PART OF THE SPELLING.

That is the point. English is a case-sensitive language. "Danish" is
a language. "danish" is a pastry. "Polish" is a nationality. "polish"
is how you get silverware clean. "Turkey" is a country. "turkey" is
a bird", and so on. If they wanted to take the easy way out they could
have made it a case insensitive search and not bothered to include the
case in the dictionary, but that would be wrong. They have a "case
insensitive" mode on the software that is used to search the dictionary,
but it's not the default.

