If you look at it from a C progrtamming point of view, there are some
traditions where, for instance, #DEFINE of constants would be in uppercase,
and , historicalkly, system routines names were uppercase (although this has
changed between VAX-C and DEC-C on vax since uppercase system routines are no
longer defined in librtl.h and starlet,h)

In any event, one can use capitalisation to indicate a certain type of object
and this can improve readability of a file.

Perhaps the same could be done for files, with uppercase file names indicating
fixed non-editable files, and lowercase files being editable (config files
etc). And software created to look first at a lowercase file and then if not
found, look at the uppercase version.

So the default config for a piece of software would create an uppsercase file
name containing the default configuration and the user would create a
lowercase file of the same name with the site specific configuration.

An advantage of this would be that during upgrades, the upgrade procedure
would only touch the uppercase files, leaving the site-specific files intact.


case sensitivity *allows* for a meaning to be put to them. Whether UNIX ever
put meaning to them is another question. If there is no meaning to the case of
file names, then UNIX wasted an opportunity to make intelligent use of a feature.

On VMS, you can use logical search paths to define a directory which first
searches for the site specific files and then f not found, will look into the
base software directories. (ALL-IN-1 has taken advantage of this so that
customer's customization of the software are not zapped by upgrade procedures,
and it is possible to ALLIN1/NOCUSTOM where A1 then doesn't try to open any
site specific files (good for debugging for instance).


