"Jan-Erik S�derholm" <aaa@aaa.com> wrote in message news:3C431BFE.241A825A@aaa.com...
> And, some here seems to think that it was a *misstake* to make
> VMS case in-sensitive. But what if it wasn't :-)
>
Case-insensitive was an interpretation of an old-fashioned traditional
behavior.

>
> But, the point is that it doesn't matter realy.
>
It matters in a similar way to limiting filename lengths.   Not all
filenames are english proper nouns.   There is alot of descriptive
info (even automatically generated) that can be usefully embedded
in a filename (or directory tree.)

Not every limitation of a DEFAULT user interface need to be imposed
by the low level filesystem code (or OS kernel), where it is an olden
times UI convention and olden times filesystem limitation (e.g. SIXBIT
or RAD50) that caused the effective case folding.   Attempts
to supply similar behavior along with the bonus of some UC/LC
support is what probably caused the mishmash of UC/LC support
in latter-day DEC OSes.

It is better to compare DIFFERENT filenames as being different.   Folded
cases in the filesystem (or filesystem support) code is a committee-type
compromise, but not because of OS/filesystem design needs (like SIXBIT
might be),  but because of UI layer desires.

John




