On Thu, 10 Jan 2002, Jan C. [iso-8859-1] Vorbr=FCggen wrote:
> You don't seem to understand we want a case-preserving, case-insensitive
> system - just about the only thing NTFS got right, with ODS-5 following
> suit. Capitalization is an aid to text comprehension, but it changes the
> meaning only in very rare cases. _That_ is the proper analogy.

Yes, I appreciate the NTFS behavior too.  But you are missing the point.

Equivalency between a UC and LC character is contextual.  It depends upon
the language, and sometimes the region, of a particular individual.

Think about this carefully.  This means that if you presume case
independence, there are name string pairs that refer to *different* names
in one place, but the *same* name in another place.

It is therefore impossible to have case independent file names without
doing one of the following:
 1) have inappropriate case matches in some regions
 2) have inappropriate case non-matches in some regions
 3) have non-portability of names between regions since the filesystem
    is different for each region.

This situation comes up even with the Latin script.

Furthermore, as you acknowledge, there are instances in which
capitalization does change the meaning.

-- Mark --

