here
> *is* an uppercase derivitive of it, will it match the lowercase version.

Exactly!  And you are stuck with implementing one of three choices:
 a) inappropriate matching
 b) inappropriate non-matching
 c) non-portable matching

There is also the choice of "only match the 26 basic Latin alphabetics and
the hell with anything else", but that's really a form of choice (b) so I
don't list it separately.

> Unfortunately, outside of say the simple ASCII or ISO-Latin 1 sets, case
> rules are not a simple bit set or bit clear.

They aren't even clear within Latin 1, as the next comment points out:

> Your last example really points out some interesting areas of thought.
> Ideally, I suppose =DF should match SS - if your system language was Germ=
an.

Well, does ess-tset match SS or not?

I can come up with an example, based upon which of the three choices above
that you pick, for why you did the wrong thing.

(c) seems to be the most attractive choice, until you realize how vitally
important portability really is.  (c) is the choice that NTFS made, and I
have encountered the portability problems!!!

The reason for case-sensitivity is not because of an evil desire to screw
people who are accustomed to case-insensitivity.  It's because when you
look at the larger issue, there is no way (and I do mean no way) to
implement case-insensitivity without doing the wrong thing to someone.

More specifically, there is no way to make case-insensitivity be
fully appropriate, never inappropriate, and completely portable.  You have
to compromise on at least one of these.

Once you make the difficult choice to be case-sensitive, you don't have to
deal with these problems.  You have appropriate and predictable behavior
that is completely portable.  What you don't have is a match between
"foo.bar" and "FOO.BAR".

