In article
<Pine.NXT.4.50.0201101329590.2525-100000@Tomobiki-Cho.CAC.Washington.EDU>,
Mark Crispin  <mrc@CAC.Washington.EDU> wrote:
: ...
: Think about this carefully.  This means that if you presume case
: independence, there are name string pairs that refer to *different* names
: in one place, but the *same* name in another place.
: 
: It is therefore impossible to have case independent file names without
: doing one of the following:
:  1) have inappropriate case matches in some regions
:  2) have inappropriate case non-matches in some regions
:  3) have non-portability of names between regions since the filesystem
:     is different for each region.
: 
Welcome to the new world of computing as we never knew it.  Matters are
even worse than Mark says when you consider that the future direction is
for all computer text to converge on Unicode (normally UTF-8 on the wire),
in which:

 . A single character such as a-grave may be represented either as
   one code point (precomposed) or two (base letter plus combining
   diacritic) (this doesn't sound too bad until you think about
   Vietnamese...)

 . Every character must be looked up in a database to find out if it
   is a standalone character or a combining character, and what its
   case mappings are (and even then, as Mark says, this is language
   and region dependent).

 . Since combining characters *follow* base characters, you never know
   what the current character is supposed to be until you have looked
   ahead to the next (and maybe the ones after that).

 . Combining sequences must be "normalized", which involves database
   lookups and sorting.

 . Lots of characters look exactly alike, such as Latin A, Greek A,
   and Cyrillic A.  Imagine the possibilities for mischief.

As confusing as this sounds, it's actually LESS confusing than the
current situation, in which you can't tell what character set was used
to create a file or file name, and therefore you haven't a clue about
how to convert the case.  To illustrate: a shared Unix system might be
used by a German and a Russian.  The German sets her terminal emulator 
for Latin-9, the Russian for KOI-8R.  How is the OS going to case-convert
the filenames?  If all filenames are UTF-8 *by definition*, then at least
there's a chance.

Bottom line: computing was ridiculously easy for us English speakers
(and ridiculously hard for everybody else) up til now, but those days
are rapidly drawing to a close.  It's only a matter of time before
"hello world" takes several thousand lines of source code.

- Frank


