> for all computer text to converge on Unicode (normally UTF-8 on the wire),
> in which:
>
>  . A single character such as a-grave may be represented either as
>    one code point (precomposed) or two (base letter plus combining
>    diacritic) (this doesn't sound too bad until you think about
>    Vietnamese...)

An excellent example of a situation where pure binary look-up mechanisms
produce even more confusing behavior than case-sensitivity produces:  two
strings *display* absolutely identically on the screen or page but don't
match on look-up (because they formed those same characters using different
byte sequences).

>
>  . Every character must be looked up in a database to find out if it
>    is a standalone character or a combining character, and what its
>    case mappings are (and even then, as Mark says, this is language
>    and region dependent).

Exactly.  But (as I said elsewhere) this discussion isn't about
implementation difficulty but about what the 'right' approach is.

>
>  . Since combining characters *follow* base characters, you never know
>    what the current character is supposed to be until you have looked
>    ahead to the next (and maybe the ones after that).

Again, an implementation issue:  there's no ambiguity in what the name
eventually turns out to be.

>
>  . Combining sequences must be "normalized", which involves database
>    lookups and sorting.

Let's not get unduly pessimistic here:  the implementation *is* feasible,
though not dirt-simple.  The most direct approach is to convert all names on
input to a single, normalized form for look-up (preserving the user-provided
name separately for presentation and other purposes).  Relatively few
characters trigger significant exceptional handling, so while everyone pays
code space for it only those languages that are 'difficult' pay much in
processing overhead (and not that much even then, compared with the other
overheads of a look-up).

>
>  . Lots of characters look exactly alike, such as Latin A, Greek A,
>    and Cyrillic A.  Imagine the possibilities for mischief.

Exactly - again, an instance where they should all match on look-up if they
appear identical on the screen/page.

>
> As confusing as this sounds, it's actually LESS confusing than the
> current situation, in which you can't tell what character set was used
> to create a file or file name, and therefore you haven't a clue about
> how to convert the case.  To illustrate: a shared Unix system might be
> used by a German and a Russian.  The German sets her terminal emulator
> for Latin-9, the Russian for KOI-8R.  How is the OS going to case-convert
> the filenames?  If all filenames are UTF-8 *by definition*, then at least
> there's a chance.

Yup - either per-name character-set specification (which doesn't allow
mixed-set names) or UNICODE/UTF-8 is necessary (probably the latter is
preferable if it is now sufficiently mature to be reasonably acceptable
world-wide).

- bill





