Hello folks:

Today I uploaded a latest snapshot to my ftp site.  Good news!  Now I 
successfully installed NetBSD/vax into RD54 disk image with selected three 
sets - netbsd, base, and etc sets.  Installation took estimated 10 to 15 
minutes to complete.  I sucessfully booted NetBSD from RD54 through 
system initialization and startup scripts into the Login prompt.

However a few problems still need fixed.  During startup scripts, I got
several segmentation fault errors but boot went so well.  When I attempted 
to execute 'ps -ax', I got "Illegal Instruction".  But, 'ps -a' worked 
fine.  I believe option '-x' cause 'Illegal Instruction'.  I am trying
figure out why... 

I was able edit files by using 'vi' command.  I added my own account and 
was able logged into system with it.

OpenVMS still crashes the same.

Well, I implemented all floating instructions execpt H_floating and 
debugged F_floating instructions by using KA630-A 1.3 firmware (during 
countdown #4).  I will debug rest of them by using HCORE tests soon.
That's why newfs command in NetBSD wants floating instructions to execute
(both F_floating and D_floating).

I fixed 'disasm' command so that you now can display assembly on your 
panel terminal instead of always going to debug.log.  I switched to 
socket-based file I/O accesses so that same disasm routines can send 
assembly lines to debug.log or user's terminal screen.

I rewrote memory accesses to speed up VAX emulator and it now is slightly
faster than VAX 8600 on 1.9 GHz Pentium 4 system.  Debug.log showed that
4.4 MIPS (VUP 4.4) was increased from 2.3 MIPS in pervious version.
When countdown reached zero, it disabled parity tests to speed up.

For next release, I will add larger disks into RQDX3 emulation that NetBSD 
requires for full installation.  Breakpoint systems, examine/deposit, etc 
would be added.  I know that debug facility needs improvement in my 
emulator.  All rest of floating instructions will be debugged by using 
HCORE tests.

-- Tim Stark

