 Robert> to the "your system ate my data -- get it back!" type demands.

The point that I called you on was this:

  robert> Limiting EXPUNGE (or equivalent) to user-initiated *only*,
  robert> is a 'luxury'.  It requires additional resources to support.
  robert> In systems of the '70s, and early '80s, the incremental
  robert> cost _was_ "non-trivial".  A user transiting from a system
  robert> with the luxuries to one without them -- even if the hardware
  robert> and O/S was the same, was in for some "rude suprises".

The example you have provided fails to address that, because it is
about users transitioning to a system that's configured to remove their
files automatically, when the old system did not do that.  The fact
that the two systems were both running TOPS-20 is not relevent,
because any operating system can be configured to randomly delete
people's files (through a periodic batch job, or on many systems,
by manual human "cleaning up" activity).    That's a comment on the
policies of how the machine is configured to operate, not on whether
the particular feature I cited in the operating system works very well.

TOPS-20, the example that you keep citing here, can be configured to
automatically "expunge" files in various circumstances, and if you
configure it that way, it doesn't meet the critera that I specified:
the system never expunges files except at user request.

 Robert> See above.  specifically files in 'DELETEd, but not EXPUNGEd'
 Robert> status for _days_ (University, ran EXPUNGE once over the
 Robert> weekend), vs 'gone almost immediately' (service-bureau --
 Robert> system-wide EXPUNGE ran every 5-10 minutes, regular schedule,
 Robert> *no* warning).  In addition for service bureau, EXPUNGE (per
 Robert> user) ran on session logout.  Customer has two sessions
 Robert> logged-in, and (accidentally) deletes file in one session,
 Robert> Employee using other session -ust-happens- to log out before
 Robert> the guy in the first session can finish typing in the
 Robert> UNDELETE.  guess what??  wanna take bets on who they blame?

Your other example above is, again, mostly about the policies chosen
for a system: when will it automatically expunge files (which is
specifically not what I was talking about).  But the second half of
it could sort of be construed to address the question.  Let me get
this: you have more than one person simultaneously logged into
the same user account, and they are sabotaging each other by
deleting files out from underneath each other... and this is 
