Based on my experience in technical support, there are many variants of this
scenario depending on the user interface:

	1. The second user uses a GUI to select the file. She uses the same
	   filename with the same case settings, no problem. This person
	   doesn't care if the file system is case sensitive, case insensitive
	   for ASCII-only, case-insensitive for Latin-1, or completely i18n
	   compliant.

	2. The second user uses an environment where she provides the name
	   without realtime feedback. Traditional UNIX/VMS, or she cuts and
	   pastes the name in a GUI, etc...

	   2a. The second user decides she got the name wrong, looks it up,
	       and enters the right one. This person may gripe about the
	       extra step, but she's still going to end up where she needs
	       to be.

	   2b. The second user just goes ahead and uses the name anyway.

THIS is the real problem. Now we have two files that both have the
same case-insensitive representation, AND we have users who "know"
the IS is case-insensitive. They continue to update the same file
out of sync.

And don't tell me this won't happen. The program may present her the file
name as a default, and create it if it doesn't exist. Even if it pops up
a dialog box users frequently just say "OK".

>I would be interested in an example where two languages have a lower-case
