I missed the beginning of this thread, so forgive me if I'm restating
what you already know: he's not describing case-bashing, nor case-insensitive. 
Case preserving means that the file system remembers what the name looked like
when you created it, but is case-insensitive on reference.  So when you list
your directory, you'll see "FooBAR", but you can refer to it using any case.
That is probably what you did mean -- it's what Windows NT does -- but I just
wanted to clarify what he's asking for.

For the kernel/filesystem itself, I don't know why it would be any
harder to change in Unix than any other operating system that already
supports mixed-case file names.  But Unix, especially, is a collection
of programs, and maybe they have some kind of assumptions that would
require them all to be modified, too. Maybe application programs, also.
There might also be problems transitioning from one kind of system to
the other, or interoperating (eg. over NFS) between different systems.
I don't know exactly what all the problems might be, or how they might
be easily or not so solvable.   Perhaps Fred or someone will elaborate.

