escape mechanisms
> are needed to support the superset.

That is not the issue under discussion:  I already agreed above that
handling mixed-case names is desirable, and supporting them in no way
requires lookup case-sensitivity.

   This unnecessarily lengthens filenames when
> full upper/lower case is desirable.   Not only that, a fully escaped
filename will likely
> have more length limitations!!!
>
> There is NO REASON to fix that limitation deep in the OS/Filesystem code,
when the
> limitation can be programmed at the user level.

There are very good reasons to make such limitations intrinsic:

1.  This standardizes the system's behavior across applications.

2.  This conforms to the ways people usually use names (I'll leave aside the
question of whether programmers are people, since I'm not interested in
discussing the pros and cons of case-sensitive computer language identifiers
here:  as I said above, computer languages are not file systems).

Yes, some languages don't use the concept of case - but we're clearly not
talking about those here because with them there is no issue.  And indeed
case or similar concepts aren't always easy to be insensitive to in some
languages (witness the complexities of Unicode collation), but the
discussion here is not apparently about implementation difficulty.

It is not sensible to argue for case-sensitivity in file names without
arguing for support of arbitrary binary byte strings (or even bit strings)
in file names.  Such an argument presupposes that the primary use of file
names is by entities other than human beings.  While such non-human entities
are welcome to use file systems, I don't believe file systems should be
designed for their convenience at the expense of ours, especially as most
such non-human use that can't easily use case-insensitive names (e.g., using
decimal numbers as names avoids case issues entirely) tends to be using the
file system as a substitute for some more appropriate tool.

