Answer two questions:

Q1. Who is going to choose and use those names in almost all cases?

A1. The human user of the system. A program would do just was well with
    an arbitrary (say 128-bit pseudo-random) number, or some index        
intrinsic to the system (an inode in Unix, an FID in VMS, the index
    into the symbol table in a computer language).

Q2. What suits a human user best, what reduces his error rate the most?
    This is a human factors question that has little to do with computer
    science or engineering.

A2. Names should
    - be hierarchical
    - have a redundant representation
    - use soft/fuzzy matches

Case-sensitive lookups violate the last two items.

