In that case, a pure binary comparison of filenames is probably best.
This allows for those who always use UC or LC to get their
expected results, this allows for extended character sets to
work (at the OS/filesystem level), and supports the best
flexibility.

Filenames are indeed artifacts of a tradition.   The tradition convieniently
removed case (because of SIXBIT/RAD50), and CLIs/OS/Library code
would implicitly normalize because of the conversion.   The need for such
is LONG GONE nowadays.   More advanced OSes can even support multi
byte chars in names (to remove the roman character set provincialism.)

As a minimum, removing the UC/LC case insensitivity provincialism is
a good thing.

I won't argue for case sensivitivty on Tops10, but those were the days
where memory was VERY EXPENSIVE.   That choice was made approx
40yrs ago, and technology has moved way beyond.   Even expected UI
has changed significantly.

John




