Has anyone else suffered premature alignment failure of TZ89 type
drives or libraries. We have two TL891 mini-libraries - one on VMS and
one on an NT server. Both have now suffered failures requiring a drive
replacement within 2 years. The VMS system drive had been in use 5
hours a day for 18 months = 5 *365*1.5 which is less than 3000 hours.
Two cartridge swaps per day. The NT system runs for around 12 hours
per day and failed in 1 year with the same symptoms. Auto-calibrate
sees an existing tape as blank. During a recent CDR test the recovery
specialists said they commonly saw alignment problems with
TZ89/DLT7000 drives.

As MTBF is quoted at 30,000 tape motion hours it would appear we have
had two drives fail at around 1/10th that figure (the VMS systems
drive the tape continuously, NT stop/starts so the NT system also
failed after something of the order of 3,000 hours).

Anyone else seen similar? Note you will never see this problem until
you try and read an old tape.
--
Alan


