Please quote my entire response for context:

Yes it does...   The mistaken tradition of normalizing the display of
filenames to a fixed case (e.g. RAD50/SIXBIT) might have given rise
to the mistaken notion that filename comparison should be case insensitive.

Even VMS by default, normalizes the case on the command line?   There
is no valid reason for that.   A proper design would allow for the specification
of case insensitivity when desired, rather than fixing that behavior.

Of course, there is no sense in doing it today.   Those who don't want to
UC/LC their filenames don't have to.   Under UNIX, for example, if you
use the default LC, then that is what you get.   There is no need to
do anything other than binary comparison of filenames, for UI reasons
(unless the UI wants to modify the behavior), or for the purpose of
automated filename generation.

Anyone who believes that capitalization isn't part of standard usage probably
believes that ee cummings writing style is normal.

John



