I should clarify that the 'tradition' of normalizing the case is NOT THE SAME
THING as the 'tradeoff.'   It should be considered that case insensitivity is
an in-between implementation of single case filenames and full support
for upper/lower case.   The 'tradition' of case insensitivity is a mistaken
design tradeoff that derives from previous lack of case and some 'justifications'
that case sensitivity is undesirable.   Fortunately, the least common
denominator is much higher than it used to be, and case sensitivity is a
minor lesson for those who don't initially understand it, and natural for
those who understand the fact that a name is ONLY a name composed
of distinct representations of characters.   Such names
can be expressed in English, roman characters, or non-roman characters.
Filenames can be considered to be proper names, arbitrary sequences or
even computer generated tags.   A filename need not be constrained
to be a single word, but could even be a full name including punctuation
(within design tradeoffs of length or character set.)

If, for UI purposes, filenames are desired to be limited to simple one
word UC/LC insensitive names, then it can be implemented as such.  However,
imposing that limitation at the OS/filesystem level is violating simple
layering concepts.

Again, if the folding of UC to LC is made as a matter of a design tradeoff,
then it should be accepted as a 'tradeoff.'   Oh so many times, a tradeoff
is proclaimed a 'feature'.   Frankly, old UNIX filenames and systems that
didn't support much beyond English characters were only a step more
evolved than SIXBIT or RAD50.

Maintaining a tradition IS NOT a justification for maintaining a limitation
at the engineering or development level.   At the marketing/political/advocacy
level, then any limitation (e.g. case insensitivity of one sort or another)
could be construed to be an advantage :-).

