    I'd note that "almost every doco" is irrelevant.  The VMS documentation,
specifically, the DCL Help entries for COPY, RENAME and DELETE (as a sampling
of file related commands) NEVER refer to "wildcard expansion".  The entries do
use the phrase "specify the names of one or more files", but more specifically
refer to "file specifications" and the use of wildcard characters in various
fields of file specifications.  NEVER, NEVER, NEVER do they say "wildcard
expansion", or that being equivalent to a "list of files".  And for good
reason: it would be wrong to do so.


> It isn't consistant to use the same kind of spec for a 'list of names' being
> virtual or literal and also as the transformation grammar.

    Fine, not relevant.  If you didn't have unix blinders on, you wouldn't
have such a problem with the concepts (like the difficulty in learning a
new language with an entirely different grammar than you're used to).  In
context (i.e., usage within the language), DCL wildcard semantics are entirely
consistent, just not what YOU are used to.

    I'll offer just one observation: wildcards in DCL have the semantics of
"match something I care not to explicitly type".  This allows much broader
use AND tailoring to particular contexts than would a more RESTRICTED
semantics that said BOTH "match file names" and "expand to a list".  That's
why it's natural in DCL to use wildcards not just in file applications, but
also with queues, symbols, logical names, and a bunch of other things I can't
think of at the moment.  To a DCL user, this seems HIGHLY consistent.  Indeed,
the inability to use a wildcard to specify, e.g., a print queue name to 'lpq'
seems highly inconsistent and annoying...

    -Ken
