"Bob Koehler" <koehler@encompasserve.org> wrote in message news:8JGczAE44eCr@eisner.encompasserve.org...
> In article <PEL_7.23$vb1.2545@news1.iquest.net>, "JD" <dyson@jdyson.com> writes:
> 
> > Using the standard argument mechanism, the '-t' is a switch.
> 
>    There is no standard argument mechansim.  If there was tar cxv 
>    would not be the same as tar -cxv .
> 
There are several exceptions to the rule.   This is historical practice and
one major reason for the creation of getopt.   There is some historical
compatibility (where dd is probably the most egregious.), but I haven't
written anything that parses args that uses the techniques of 'dd'.   Note
that it is MUCH HARDER to use the 'dd' type rules rather than just use the
builtin mechnanism.

I am sure that on DCL systems you can get direct access to the command
line, and use non-standard parsing, can't you?   I'd hate to use a system
that wouldn't allow for some bypassing the default parsing, because there
are SOME CASES where that might be useful.   Perhaps a limited JCL scheme
where you have to add // in front of every command to show 'discipline'
would make those who love modal mechanisms much happier :-).   DCL
is already modal enough :-).

This doesn't argue against the 'standard argument mechanism', because it
was created to address issues of switch incompatibility.   People really do
use it also.

John



