In message <pPY_7.47$vb1.10190@news1.iquest.net>, "JD" <dyson@jdyson.com> writes:
>DCL BY DEFAULT has inconsistant parsing of wildcards in differing contexts
                                                         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
>as NORMAL behavior.

There's nothing wrong with parsing wildcards differently in different contexts,
it's only a problem if you parse them different in the same context.  When
you say "show queue hp*", you aren't looking up any files, the context at the
point where it sees "hp*" is for queue name.

DCL doesn't process wildcard characters at all other than the cases where the
command tables give it hint that a wildcard character is inappropriate inside
a particular parameter.

The underlying assumption about all this consistency stuff is that people
are going to look a command line the way a unix shell does, which is true
for people writing shell commands.  If you use DCL, you are going to
look at command lines the way DCL does.



