"David Jones" <JONESD@er6.eng.ohio-state.edu> wrote in message
news:a1ho3q$7du$1@charm.magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu...
> In message <pPY_7.47$vb1.10190@news1.iquest.net>, "JD" <dyson@jdyson.com> writes:
> >DCL BY DEFAULT has inconsistant parsing of wildcards in differing contexts
>                                                          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
> >as NORMAL behavior.
>
> There's nothing wrong with parsing wildcards differently in different contexts,
> it's only a problem if you parse them different in the same context.
>
That is a matter of YOUR definition (and probably those who like the inconsistant
parsing of wildcards depending on context.)   If you want to use a wild card as
a 'list of files' or 'expression specifying a target name transformation', then that
defacto demonstrates that the semantics are inconsistant.

Frankly, I used to like the DCL scheme also, and wrote a DCL before RSX11M had
one normally available (from scratch, as a CLI.)   After that, I learned the shell
approach, and was still biased towards the 'DCL' approach.   Even with the
skeptcism and preference for the 'DCL' inconsistancy, I found the shell approach
to be 'cleaner'.   Eventually, as I learned (at a technology, not an engineering level)
the relative advantages, the shell won out.

It has been many years since that happened, and the full context of my thinking
back in the early '80's is hard to re-construct, but the shell approach has pretty
much taken over, and is VERY consistant.   There are indeed abnormal cases
where both schemes can be tripped up, but IMO, consistant semantics win.

If DCL 'rename' was well designed and consistant, it would be something like
this:   rename /newext:x *.c.   Perhaps the transformation wouldn't be specified
in the same way as a filespec.   There is a BIG difference between a transformation
and 'names'.

John



