> So the fault on (R2)+ would unwind the increment on R1? Or is the post-
> increment deferred until after the instruction is complete? What about
> Autodecrement Indexed mode?

That is implementation-defined - it just has to appear to any outside observer
(including the access violation exception handler aka the page
fault handler) that the register changes have not occured. The complexity
of this (it's even worse for some things on the 68040) is what puts the
VAX on the top of the CISC list in John Mashey's processor classification.
Think an ADDP6 with all bells and whistles...IIRC, it needs something like 53
pages in the worst case to execute.

> Is that still true on heavily pipelined VAXes?

Yes, because it's architecturally mandated.

	Jan


