 . be able to create a file "23" and a file "23/07"
 . be able to create a file "23/"

In the case of VMS, however, we're talking about an arbitrary prohibition
of a character in a filename for reasons unrelated to the filesystem.

The fallacy in prohibiting the use of a character in a filesystem due to
the dictates of a user interface is that it presumes that all access to
the filesystem is through that user interface.  That necessarily makes
that filesystem less capable, and in client/server applications that lack
of capability is deadly.

The UNIX filesystem is deficient enough with its theft of "/" for a
directory delimiter and NUL-termination.  But at least those deficiencies
have a legitimate filesystem-based reason, and aren't due to the whims of
a command decoder.

-- Mark --

