> back in the early '80's is hard to re-construct, but the shell approach
has pretty
> much taken over, and is VERY consistant.   There are indeed abnormal cases
> where both schemes can be tripped up, but IMO, consistant semantics win.
>
> If DCL 'rename' was well designed and consistant, it would be something
like
> this:   rename /newext:x *.c.   Perhaps the transformation wouldn't be
specified
> in the same way as a filespec.   There is a BIG difference between a
transformation
> and 'names'.

This discussion seems much akin to debating how many angels can dance on the
head of a pin, but perhaps I'm missing something.

Alternatively, perhaps the problem is that the same syntax is being used for
different purposes without adequate explanation - i.e., that in the command
rename *.c *.x the second argument LOOKS like a 'wildcard specification' but
in fact is not one (any more than '-' in a Unix command always announces a
switch).

If one adopts this attitude, there's no inconsistency in DCL's treatment,
just a possibly-confusing use of identical constructs with
positionally-dependent meaning (but *any* operation which accepts, say, both
an input spec and an output spec often uses the same constructs for both but
with different meanings).  But since those who actually use this syntax
don't seem to be confused by it, if there's no issue of semantic purity
involved then is there any real problem?

- bill





