>
> However, a file system is *truly* broken if it *distinguishes* between upper
> and lower case in file names (rather than just preserving the case the name
> was created with and performing case-insensitive look-ups).  Unix has been
> broken in this fashion since Day 1.
>
Distingushing upper/lower case is GOOD.   Many computer languages do, and
it is a superset to be able to distingush.   If any given userland program doesn't
want to distingush, then there are ways to do that on good OSes (including
at the command level.)

If an OS (or filesystem) is restricted to UPPER CASE only, then silly escape mechanisms
are needed to support the superset.   This unnecessarily lengthens filenames when
full upper/lower case is desirable.   Not only that, a fully escaped filename will likely
have more length limitations!!!

There is NO REASON to fix that limitation deep in the OS/Filesystem code, when the
limitation can be programmed at the user level.

John



