I wrote:
> Yes, well, TOPS-10 allowed any 36-bit word to be used as a base filename,
> and any 18-bit halfword *except* "SFD" (encoded in sixbit) as an
> extension.

peter@taronga.com (Peter da Silva) writes:
> What were the other 18 bits?

The other 18 bits in the directory entry were the pointer to the RIB
(Retrieval Information Block).  The RIB is like a Unix inode, except
that they can be in any cluster (or was it supercluster?) on the disk.
This sets an absolute upper bound of 2**18 (256K) files on a disk.

A multi-disk structure can have more files since each disk has the
directory entries for the file on that disk.

The MFD (Master File Directory, equivalent to a Unix root directory)
contains one directory entry for each UFD (User File Directory),
including one for the MFD itself.  A UFD "filename" is a PPN
(Project/Programmer Number) in octal.  It is presented to the user
as the octal PPN, rather than as the sixbit characters in which a
filename is normally displayed/entered.

I seem to recall that PIP (Peripheral Interchange Program) allowed
some means for the entry of filenames and extensions in octal, which
was useful if you accidentally created a file with an unusual name
and needed to rename or delete it.  The typical example is a filename
beginning with one or more space characters.

[This is from memory of using the stuff 21 years ago, so I may well
have the details wrong.]


