>storage space and high activity levels would be far less likely to be able
>to recover a deleted file than others).  Is that really a problem in this
>case?

Short answer:   yes.  *IF* the users come to rely on the 'normal' behavior
of a system with lots of free-space, and low activity levels, they _will_
have some *very*rude* surprises when they move to a high-activity/low-free-
space system.  Reduction-to-absurdity example.  they want to see what's
in the directory *other* than a specific type of file.  so they delete
all of that type, do a listing, and 'undelete'.  *EXPECTING* to get everything
back.  Guess what happens on a resource-starved system?   And, believe me,
users *do* do this kind of 'silliness'.

People do -not- build patterns of action based on the _defined_ system 
behavior.  but the "percieved" behavior.   And fault the system _design_
when reality differs from those perceptions.


