Yes, actually two solutions here:

The Logical Company (Cottage Grove,OR) has an IEEE488 PCI controller (DCI
3100) which we support. It is a clone of the IEQ11 (except for the PCI bus),
so that the existing VMS drivers work, they think they see a standard IEQ11.
They developed more of these cards for CHARON-VAX (e.g. DCI 1100 for DRV11
or DR11-C replacement).

General PCI devices could be made visible in the emulated environment, but
existing VAX drivers would not know how to work with them, and they would
have to have at least Qbus like registers to be usable.

The same company sells a Qbus adapter (BCI 2104), a connection between a PCI
slot and an existing Qbus backplane. Any physical Qbus peripheral on that
backplane is added transparently to the peripherals emulated by CHARON-VAX,
and existing drivers work. This way you can boot for instance from a
(non-emulated) Qbus disk controller.

