>He wants to delete the *.c files that have a corresponding *.x
>file (the same "something" in both cases).

If that's what he means, then you'd have to write a little DCL.  (Not 
tested, sorry.)  This'll find every *.c file, delete it, and delete the 
corresponding .x file if there is one.  Depending on whether you care
about matching the version numbers, etc, you may need to make additional
changes.  This constructs the .X file name and checks to see that it exists;
you could also $ SET NOON and just DELETE it, which will fail harmlessly if
the file isn't there, but that will produce ugly error messages unless you
$ SET MESSAGE to turn it all off, and I'd rather not keep turning them off
and on again.

$ START:
$   CFILE  = F$SEARCH ("*.c;*",1) 
$   IF (CFILE .EQS. "") THEN EXIT  ! didn't find any more
$   DELETEX/NOCONFIRM 'CFILE
$   ! If you want to get only the corresponding .x version for each .c 
$   ! version, the command below will do it; otherwise do 
$   ! ".X;*" and leave off the second F$PARSE
$   XFILE = F$PARSE(CFILE,,,"NAME") + ".X" + F$PARSE(CFILE,,,"VERSION")
$   XFILE = F$SEARCH (XFILE,2)
$   IF (XFILE .EQS. "") THEN GOTO START
$   DELETEX/NOCONFIRM 'XFILE
$   GOTO START


-- Alan


