I think you are missing two things here...

1. Command lines parameters.

- The DIRECTORY command takes *one* parameter. That parameter
  should be the file or files to be listed. The paramater could
  a single valid VMS file spec (HOST"User
pw"::dev:[dir]name.ext;version)
  with wildcards and defaults in apropriate places, or a number of
  file specifications separated with a comma ",". It's still *one*
  single parameter.

- The COPY command takes two paramaters. The input file spec and
  the output file spec. The first paramater (input file spec) could
  be a single file spec or a combination of multiple file specifications
  separated by a comma ",". It's still only *two* parameters as seen by
  COPY.EXE.

2. Defaulting a missing version to <all versions>

- The DIRECTORY command will accept a file specification without
  a specific version. This will defaults to <all versions>.
  So "DIRECTORY *.C" is valid.

- The DELETE command either take a ";*" version wildcard to mean
  all versions, or any of the other ways of specifying absolute
  or rellative versions numbers.
  So "DELETE *.C" is invalid.


So, "*.c *.x" is valid syntax to those commands that takes two
input parameters and where these parameters dont need to have
version numbers. I can think of COPY, APPEND and DIFFERENCES.

"*.c *.x" is invalid syntax if the command do *not* accept two
parameters.

Simple, isn't it :-)

And if foo is some program you have written, you'll get exactly
"*.c *.x", and it's up to foo to do whatever it want with it...

