Christopher Stacy  <cstacy@spacy.Boston.MA.US> wrote:
>All this ignorant talk about the imagined problems with operating
>systems whose DELETE commands simply mark the entry as "deleted", 
>but only free the space of such files when a seperate EXPUNGE 
>command are used...is hillarious, or sad.  There are decades of actual
>experience with such operating systems, of which TOPS-20 was only one.
>They worked great!
>

In general, _most_ operating systems "worked great".  DELETE/EXPUNGE has
nothing intrinsicly wrong with it.  It _is_ a nice feature when the *only*
way the expunge happens is when the user expressly initiates it.  When it
_can_ happen automatically/unintentionally (e.g. at session log-out), or
be independantly initiated by the O/S, 'in time of need',  It does result
in a degree of 'unpredictability' in system behavior.  And users *will*
be unhappy when that unpredictability does happen to bite -them-.  And 
will blame the system, not their own goof-up.  Limiting EXPUNGE (or equivalent)
to user-initiated *only*, is a 'luxury'.  It requires additional resources
to support. In systems of the '70s, and early '80s, the incremental cost
_was_ "non-trivial".   A user transiting from a system with the luxuries
to one without them -- even if the hardware and O/S was the same, was in
for some "rude suprises".


Christopher, your statement of "ignorant talk about the imagined problems.."
demonstrates *your* arrogance, ignorance, and lack of breadth of experience.

I have used, and supported users on, a *wide* variety of systems, super-
computers to micros, and all levels inbetween.  Various of which -have- had
EXPUNGE/DELETE, or equivalents, and others which have worked on the basis
of 'you said it, you meant it, *now*'.  I have "direct first-hand knowledge"
of users *doing* _every_one_ of the various kinds of dumb things I have 
mentioned.  It really *IS* true that "for every fool-proof system, there 
exists a sufficiently-determined fool capable of breaking it."  





