Which confirms that the VMS/DCL interpretation of wildcards is as inconsistant
as the programmer(s) care to make it.  "directory entries", "related names",
or "something (anything) else".

And, that there is *NO*WAY* -- from inspection of the command-line only -- to
determine which interpretation applies, to which instance.  Specific knowledge
of the particular context is required. 

The semantics of the wildcard construct depend utterly on the context in which
it is employed.

For commands bundled with VMS or built into DCL, there has been effort made
to classify various instances of wildcard availability into groups, and to
ensure that within those groups that the wildcard behaves in the same manner.
The authors *did* do a very good job of limiting the number of distinct
behavior groups, and using 'intuitively obvious' selection of which behvior
was to apply in various contexts.as to apply in various contexts.

User-written commands _can_, but DO NOT HAVE TO employ the same semantics
as *any* of those employed by system-supplied commnds.  "foo *.x *.y" is 
free to use one of the strings '*.x' or '*.y' as, for example, a pattern
of text to search for, in the list of files represented by the other string.




