Actually, most places I've been where '/' is the switch character you
can't use it in filenames; if you see a '/' you know it's not a filename.

The difficulty with Unix isn't so much using '-' as the switch character as 
it is accepting just about bloody anything in a filename. I think the only 
think you _can't_ put in a Unix filename is '/' and possibly 0. Quotes, 
spaces, backsplashes, and the switch character are all OK.
