Sure, but maybe what I typed was "foo *". 

Or, perhaps, what happened is that I bought a new disk and want to move
all the files from my home directory to it. "cp -R -p ~/*" doesn't work
because it doesn't get files and directories starting with '.'.
Following that up with "cp -R -p ~/.*" doesn't work, because it starts
walking down "./.." and trying to pick everything up. And I do have a
directory starting with '.' because I've been using links which helpfully
stores bookmarks and stuff in a directory named '~/.links'.

There is, of course, "tar cvf - ~ | tar xvpf -" which A) doesn't use '-'
for its options (hello!) and 2) when it uses '-' it's not for an option!

