Perhaps, but whether "foo *" gets me in trouble depends on the filenames I
have lying around, not on the shell expansion per se. Which means I have
to keep track of an invisible mode (do I have a file starting with '-'
in this directory?) before I can reasonably do stuff. Or I have to train
myself to always type "foo ./*".

Or, perhaps, I modify the shell so when it expands "*", it expands it as
if I had typed "./*". Then at some point I'll probably have trouble like
when I was FTPing a bunch of stuff to a VMS machine and "mput *"
helpfully generated filenames as if I had typed "mput ./*". Very
annoying.

> I am surprised that no-one has complained about the
> '/' in a filename problem :-).

Because usually the guys who write the command language actually talk
to the guys writing the filesystem so the filesystem guys know not to
allow '/' in a filename to begin with.
