John Sauter <J_Sauter@Empire.Net> writes:
> The PDP-6 would interrupt an integer add instruction
> if it used indirect addressing.  Because indirect
> addressing could be chained, and the instruction
> started over on return from the interrupt, a very
> long chain of indirections would practically guarantee
> that the instruction would be interrupted by the clock,
> at least, and thus never finish.
>     John Sauter (J_Sauter@Empire.Net)

In cases of interrupts happening during indirect effective address
calculation, I think it would be more precise to say that the interrupt
aborts the instruction.

However, there were various other PDP-6 and PDP-10 instructions which
could in fact be interrupted, such that the return from the interrupt
handler would resume the execution of the interrupted instruction.
That's what the First Part Done flag is for.



