In article <a1gd48$1al$1@citadel.in.taronga.com>, peter@taronga.com (Peter da Silva) writes:
> In article <yUabDk1pELgR@eisner.encompasserve.org>,
> Bob Koehler <koehler@encompasserve.org> wrote:
>>   Uncle.  OK, I short changed that one a bit.  I know MOVCx, POLYx, and
>>   others on VAX are interruptable, but we were really talking about a
>>   simple ADD.  Do you know of any processor that has an interruptable
>>   integer ADD?
> 
> How about
> 
> 	ADDL2	(R1)+,(R2)+
> 
> where R1 and R2 both point to different non-resident pages?

I believe ADDL2 is a non-interruptable instructions on VAX,
so after the fault was handled the instruction would be
restarted from scratch.

Contrast this with MOVCx, where if the FPD (first part done)
bit has been set by the time of the interrupt, execution
after the handler exits is continued part-way through
the instruction (on whatever character it was doing, I
believe) rather than having to start all over again.
That handling based on the First Part Done bit is always
what I have thought of as being the definition of an
interruptable instruction on VAX.


