system, network, application, and research programming; also done system
management for a number of different kinds (educational/research, military,
commercial, industrial) of large and smaller sites; programmed in many
different languages, and probably used (at least a little) almost every
operating system worth mentioning in the last 29 years... I sure as hell
don't know nearly everything, but I think I can fairly be considered
to have been around the block and have both some depth and breadth.

I suppose I must lack experience relative to you: please elaborate.

  robert> Limiting EXPUNGE (or equivalent) to user-initiated
  robert> *only*, is a 'luxury'.  It requires additional resources
  robert> to support. In systems of the '70s, and early '80s, the
  robert> incremental cost _was_ "non-trivial".  A user transiting
  robert> from a system with the luxuries to one without them --
  robert> even if the hardware and O/S was the same, was in for
  robert> some "rude suprises".

If you think I was referring specifically to you as being ignorant
about how such systems work and how users behave on such operating
