>Proof positive that VMS is a piece of garbage.

Christ, Mark, that's like saying that the fact that UNIX can't make a file
called "23/07/1960" means that it's a piece of garbage. I prefer the UNIX
approach, but I can't discount the VMS way either.

It's like security. UNIX gives you the tools to build a security model that
suits your environment. VMS provides a security model that is designed to
suit a large number of environments. Which is better? Well, that depends:
at Berkeley the ability to create "group masters" so that faculty had
delegated control over their class accounts was critical. On the other
hand, VMS has the advantage that you have the same model everywhere.

