In article <l7YUUYrrH6Jp@eisner.encompasserve.org>,
Bob Koehler <koehler@encompasserve.org> wrote:
>> But there is a way to ensure that something is a filename: qualify it with
>> a path.

>   What's in a path but filenames?   Is "/a b" one file or two?

"fred" is a filename. "./fred" or /home/peter/fred" are qualified by paths.

Whether "/a b" is one file or two depends on whether the quotes are
part of the example or not.

	ls "/a b"

and
	ls /a b

obviously refer to different files.

But what I'm talking about is the point of view of the script author. The
string:

	"./$filename"

will always pass a single atomic argument to the program from any UNIX shell
(though apparently Perl doesn't always make that guarantee, which really
boggles me), and will not be interpreted as an option by any UNIX software
I know of. And that's a lot, I've used some truly oddball UNIX-inspired
systems over the past 20 years.

It *would* cause a problem on Cromemco's multi-user CP/M-compatible system
"Cromix" when running Microsoft's "M80" CP/M-based macro assembler, which
used "/" as an option character. Cromix supported VMS-style logical names
to get around this. For example, you would typically have your libraries
in "/usr/lib", so to pass that to Microsoft's linker you'd do this:

	assign M: /usr/lib
	l80 /L=M:

(It's been 20 years since I used L80, sorry if I got the syntax wrong)

I wish Microsoft had paid attention to Cromix. A super-root with assigns
instead of drive letters worked a lot better than using "\" for path
separators when you were trying (as Microsoft was at the time of DOS 2)
to merge Xenix and MS-DOS.

But Cromix is rather a special case, since it had to deal with legacy
DEC-style syntax in a UNIX-style system. I rather liked it. I do like the
idea of VMS-style logicals as an enhancement.

-- 
