In article <3c3c036b$1@news.unibe.ch>, "Stephen Ferguson" <ferguson@NOSPAMbluewin.ch> writes:
>I have a piece of medical imaging equipment which uses 5.25" MO disks for
>archiving.  The computer is a DEC Vaxstation, 1992 vintage.  The original
>documentation lists the MO as 128Mb, but the previous owner penciled in that
>it is now 650Mb.  This is all I have to go on, and I cannot contact the
>previous owner.  I have seen that TDK etc. still produce a 650Mb 5.25" MO
>cartridge, and my local computer shop still has some old MO cartridges in
>stock, but they are 650Mb per side, double-sided 1.3Gb).  Would these also
>work?

It would help to know more about the hardware (exact model number
of the drives), and if you were using Digital (as it was then)
supplied optical library software or something else to access
the media.  It might also help to know which operating system
you're using.

As a general rule, you have to get media which is hard formatted
as having 512 bytes per sector (as opposed to media which has
1024 bytes per sector, or some other value) for it to work
with VMS.  This media should still be available because I use
it (on other systems).  It's possible that other operating
systems would be happy with 1024 byte per sector media.

If the drives were designed to only work with 650 MB media (which
is quite likely if they are 1992 vintage), then they will NOT
work with higher density media.  However, I believe that the
lower density media is still available (I bought some a while
ago for my MO drive, which is of that vintage).  I don't normally
endorse products, and this is my personal opinion only, but
I've been happy with Verbatim media in my MO drive.  They should
still make what you need, but you really will have to know what
drive you have (or perhaps the exact manufactuer's ID number
of the media you are using now that works).

