OH! I get it.  he thinks rm(1) should have an 'interactive' option.
And mabe somebody should add an '-ok' option to find.
And 'noclobber' to the shell, to prevent accidental over-writing of
existing files due to output redirection.


For a *regular* (in the sense of 'frequent') user of any environment
it makes absolutely -zero- difference whether the 'default' for a 
confirmation is 'on' or 'off'.   If it is an opertion they perform
frequently, they will omit the 'ask for confirmation', or _add_ the
'dont ask me' option, as an 'automatic' part of using that command.

When people come to have an expectation that the system "will" catch
their (stupid, or otherwise) mistakes, they inevetiably end up disappointed.
They make a 'sufficiently creative' mistake, that the system does *not*
catch, and then blame the system for -not- catching it.  And there is
*NO*WAY* of telling where that 'boundary' is, untill _after_ you have
crossed it.   "For *every* fool-proof system, there exists a sufficiently
determined fool capable of breaking it"

A system that always does "_exactly_ what you told it to" is far more
_consistant_, and _predictable_ than one that tries to second-guess for
'is this reasonable, or is it a probable/likely/possible mistake'.

In human-factors studies, 'predictability' has been found to be the 
most important single factor in differentiating a good design from a
bad one.


