Bob Koehler wrote:
> 
>    Then maybe you know something I don't.  I've got this little ksh
>    function which emulates some features of DCL copy that the shell
>    won't do for me on one line.  But I can't "copy a\ a.c b", and
>    I've tried all kinds of escape and quotes with the file name a\ a.c
> 

The following works w/Solaris but should work on any system with bourne
shell. Note the double quotes and the use of $NF w/awk to pick off the 
LAST field to get the extension - this allows dots in the file name 
too (YMMV - unspecified behavior if an extension is not on the source
file).

   i=`echo "$1" | awk -F. '{print $NF}'`
   j=`echo "$2" | awk -F. '{print $2}'`
   k=`echo "$2" | awk -F. '{print $1}'`

   if [ "$j" = "" ]
   then
      to=$k.$i
   else
      to="$2"
   fi
   cp "$1" "$to"

This was tested with the command "sh a.sh a\ a.c b.c" and it copied
a file named "a a.c" to "b.c"

aak


