It looks like Peter da Silva has already done it.   If you look carefully
at what he did:  note that the names are stored into a variable, and
the careful use of the double quote.   (Thanks Peter!!!)

One thing that one should remember:   the shell will tend to split
names into an arg list.   This is true even for variables when they
are expanded.   An easy way to subvert that default behavior is to
add a double quote around a shell variable reference.  The single
quote will likely screw you in this case, because it will inhibit
expansion.

The major mistake in your script was the missing 'double quotes.'
Without those, the shell will expand the variables and try to split
the variables into arguments.

I am NOT a sysadmin or shell expert, but do kernel things.   If someone
couldn't fix the problem unless they wrote shell scripts every day,
then there might be a reason for complaint.   Fortunately, or unfortunately,
the shell very consistantly splits arguments, the double quote mechanism
is a VERY USEFUL tool.   Another hint:  don't store double quotes into
a shell variable unless there is a serious reason for it.

I suspect that things would be tricky in some cases, if a filename contains
a quoting character, but that is yet another problem.  :-).

John



