Paul Repacholi <prep@prep.synonet.com> writes:
> Apollo. The early 68000s could have faults that could not be re-started
> from the save info. Think the 020 was the first one that got it right
> enough to not need wonderous crutches...

Actually the 68010 did.  It was the first 68k to dump substantial
internal processor state onto the stack for a bus error or address error
exception, and allow instruction resumption.  The handler can either
fix the condition that caused the bus error and allow the processor
to restart the faulted memory access, or the handler can use the
address and data fields in the fault stack frame to complete or simulate
the access, then set a bit in the frame such that the processor will
consider the access to have been completed successfully.

I used the later capability to write an address error handler that
allowed executing non-word-aligned instruction streams.  Very slowly.


