I've been skimming this discussion, but I haven't seen any mention
yet of one of the original historical reasons.

Back in 1979 (more or less), I had just finished a string of three
summers working as an intern at DEC's Engineering Systems Group.  (I
believe that I was the first person outside of the VMS group to have
written a VMS device driver, during the summer of 1978.)  I arrived
at Stanford as a grad student, to discover that there was a raging
debate among a number of prominent CS faculty at various universities
over whether to run VMS or UNIX on their VAXen.  Back then, the
decision to use a VAX instead of some other hardware was apparently
a no-brainer -- with apologies to my 36-bit friends -- but the decision
about which OS to run was vigorously contentious.

The driving force behind this decision was that DARPA was then
funding major projects in VLSI and Image Understanding at several
universities.  Someone at DARPA (I forgot who) wanted all of the
VAX users on these projects to use the same OS, so that the work
could be shared more easily.

I found an old email message (6 Feb 1980) in my files that summarized
the state of things:

  In August 1979 representatives of the DARPA image understanding and
  VLSI communities met at ARPA headquarters to choose between UNIX and
  VMS.  At that time, little quantitative evidence was available:
  Berkeley's paging UNIX was not complete, and no-one in the ARPA
  community had any extensive VMS experience.  Hence, there was no way
  to evaluate relative performance of these two operating systems.

  In the absence of performance measures, it was assumed that the
  systems would be roughly comparable.  In this context, the
  recommendation was made that the community should standardize on
  UNIX, primarily on the grounds that the entire UNIX operating system
  and environment would be portable to other architectures.  The
  arguments were laid out in the Sproull report ("2nd draft of ARPA VAX
  proposal and comments on 1st draft", Sept 5, 1979, arpanet mailed
  from CMU-10A).

Since I can't remember if this message was "public" or "private"
and I can't figure out how to reach the author, I'll refrain from
naming names, or quoting from the rest of the message (the paragraphs
above being more or less opinion-free, I'll treat them as "facts").

What followed was a lengthy debate about the relative performance
of VMS and UNIX.  My recollection is hazy, and I didn't save enough
of the email messages to fill in the details (perhaps some other
pack-rat out there can provide the documents; I believe there was
a widely circulated memo by Scott Fahlman, but I can't find it).
At any rate, the original measurements showed VMS was faster in
many (but not all) ways than UNIX, but (as I recall) the UNIX camp
quickly fixed many performance bugs.  UNIX wasn't "Open Source" at
that point (AT&T's lawyers were fierce!) but the source code was
available to most or all of the DARPA-funded researchers, so it
was almost the same as Open Source; this allowed for rapid evolution
in the direction desired by these researchers.

I believe that DARPA's decision in favor of UNIX was largely based
on the portability of UNIX, and on DARPA's ability to directly control
UNIX evolution (by funding Bob Fabry's group at Berkeley, which mainly
meant Bill Joy at that point).  VMS might have had certain technical
advantages, but I think history supports DARPA's decision -- certainly
with respect to portability.

I doubt that the many reason was the cost of a VMS license.  I believe
that every VAX came with a VMS license in those days, and UNIX wasn't
free in any case (especially if you paid AT&T for a source code license).
The license costs (if I recall correctly) weren't an issue for
the DARPA researchers.

This might not explain why other VMS-centric university departments
later drifted towards UNIX.  But it has been over 20 years since the
leading CS departments in the US decided in favor of VMS, and that
was done back when DEC's support for VMS was unquestionable.

-Jeff (not speaking for Compaq, DEC, Stanford, or anyone else)

P.S.: I remember arguing forcefully (based on my rather limited experience
with both VMS and UNIX) that VMS was clearly better.  But other
people had more votes than a first-year grad student (big surprise)
and I quickly learned to type "ls" instead of "dir".


