At the risk of interjecting some concreteness into what appears to be a
primarily philosophical discussion:

On the subject of file deletion, I've often thought that deferred garbage
collection offered a kinder alternative that avoided such issues as
automatically saying 'I mean it' and then suffering later regrets.  It would
allow deleted files to be recovered for (nearly) as long as their space was
not needed elsewhere by the system (even across user logout, unlike the TOPS
example), which would give a user at least a reasonable chance of recovering
from a mistake without cluttering up the interface (i.e., delete really
means delete, but you *usually* have the opportunity to reconsider for some
period afterwards).

But it's intrinsically not predictable (e.g., a system with very little free
storage space and high activity levels would be far less likely to be able
to recover a deleted file than others).  Is that really a problem in this
case?

- bill





