> Yeah, that works real good.  Several times a week I get calls from
> users who wanted to type
>
>   tar cvf whatever.tar *.c
>
> to put their source files into a tar set.  But instead they typed
>
>   tar cvf *.c
>
> and find their first source file is now gone.
>
I almost always use rcs with an RCS subdir when writing nontrivial
code.   This provides alot of backup capability, without excess
version-itis.   Also, I have a script for vim (vi clone) that automatically
maintains last versions.   Luckily, I haven't had to access my last version
directory for the last year or so :-).   However, RCS is a useful tool
during coding and subsequent maintenance of non-trivial (but not
huge) projects.   Larger projects can take advantage of more complete
source code control (or version control.)

It is often easy to destroy recent work, no matter the OS.   Frankly,
tar is one of the programs that doesn't even adhere to the normal
switch usage.

There are often exceptions to the rules, but one cannot protect
users from self-destructive behavior.   However, the simple
expansion provided by shell, if properly explained, is alot more
consistant than the semantics provided by the program
expansion schemes.

John



