There are numerous end cases in both OSes, but when something
is as general as UNIX wrt naming, there can always be eccentric
situations.

Perhaps the most general case requires a bit more care, but using
'wildcards' with naming oddities or not knowing the names of the
files being transferred (unless it is a purely bulk transfer) is dangerous
NO MATTER THE OS.

Blanks in names are best handled with proper quoting, and the use
of a production quality shell.   There are also tricks like prefixing
filenames by './' if it is clear that it is a relative filename, in the most
general case.   Careful use of the "'" or '"' characters is also best
understood when using ksh type shells.   Using legacy shells is looking
for trouble, not because they are 'bad', but because they make
remembering the variations more difficult.   I tend to limit myself
to 'Bourne shell', but expand to 'Korn shell' when I need to.   This
is especially good now since Korn shell is available in source form.

Frankly, these are relatively non-issues, since there are people,
like me, who have had TOO MUCH zeal from both standpoints
(both the DEC and UNIX ways), and frankly, I find the UNIX way
to be more consistant.   It can bite you (and has bitten me), but
so can the DEC way.

One nice thing that has happened in NT recently is the ability to
mount filesystems below the root, so that everything is more
consistantly named.   It is probably true that VMS has added
that feature (I don't really know), but is one thing that adds
consistancy to the naming.   I don't see that filesystem namespace
segmentation is helpful at all.   This is one of the 'nits' that has
bothered me when playing with the DEC10 emulators...   BTW,
it is WONDERFUL to play with TOPS10 again, and then look
at it with many more years of experience and exposure to other
kinds of software!!!

John



