>
>to put their source files into a tar set.  But instead they typed
>
>  tar cvf *.c
>
>and find their first source file is now gone.
>
>A few (but not all) modern C compiler drivers do look at the
>command line to prevent you from typing
>
>  cc -o source.c -lm
>
>when you really meant
>
>  cc -o executable source.c -lm
>
>So the Unix user is stuck in this strange no-man's-land where
>some programs do look at the command line to prevent stupid users
>from clobbering input files just because the argument was in the
>wrong place, and other programs go ahead and do clobber the file.
>
>Of course, we're all computing professionals and none of us have
>ever made a mistake typing a command line :-).

UNIX was developed from the premise of "the user *knows* what he's doing".
and geerally makes no attempt to second-guess what the user 'intended'.

A side-effect of this is that it will "cheerfully give you enough rope to
hang yourself".  


I've used systems that second-guessed my intentions.  And would *not* let
me do things that the designer deemed was 'not reasonable'.   Almost invar-
iably, those kind of systems prevent me from doing something that I 
*delibertely* want to do, in a particular 'special set of conditions' case.
It _is_ 'probably an error', in the 'general case'. however, in =this=
instance it *is* what I want to do.  And _can't_.  I'ts just impossible.
the system *won't* let me do it.  

I *WANT* a system that _will_ "do what I tell it to,  reasonable or not".  If
I screw up -- and I *do* -- I _know_ where the fault lies.   Incidentally,
"unblunted sword" does tend to ecourage one to be much more religous about
things like _backups_.   This is not altogether a bad thing.  <grin>

For 'skilled users' -- the original target audience for UNIX -- the pro-
ductivity increase due to an O/S that a) stays out of your way, and b) just
_does_ what you tell it; it does _more_than_ offset the lost time from those
occasions where it does 'rear up and bite you'.

The 'newcomers' that are building 'reasonableness checks' into the compilers,
mentioned above, are _not_ doing their users any real favor.   There is 
established literature, in the field of user-interface design, on this point. 
Some of the early discussion/research can be found under the topic of "why 
'alias  rm   rm -i' is a Bad Idea(tm)".


