In article <uzo3ukws2.fsf@graphics.cornell.edu>, westin*nospam@graphics.cornell.edu (Stephen H. Westin) writes:
> 
> Worse. Almost all of VMS was written in VAX assembly code. To port to
> Alpha, DEC wrote a *compiler* to generate Alpha code from VAX assembly
> code. I'm sure some modules were recoded for the Alpha. But anyway,
> another compiler would need to be written to take VMS to another
> hardware platform. For starters.
> 

   Back to reality.  A great bit of VMS has since been rewritten in
   BLISS or other HLL.  I know of VAX Macro code fomr VMS 2.x that
   became BLISS by VMS 4.0.  When the VAX to Alpha port was done, DEC
   engineers claimed the VAX Macro code was down to just over 50%.

   And since the VAX instruction set was so complex, it looks like HLL
   concepts to most other systems.  Which is one of the reasons DEC was
   able to write the Macro-32 compiler for Alpha and Compaq has said
   they'll do a Macro-32 compiler for IPF.



