In article <sJsqVfwoEmbR@eisner.encompasserve.org>,
Bob Koehler <koehler@encompasserve.org> wrote:
>In article <3C369287.6080901@tsoft-inc.com>, David Froble
><davef@tsoft-inc.com> writes:
>> Actually, I'm not a HW person, and aren't really sure that an in-process 
>> instruction can be interupted.  However, there can be things delaying 
>> the next add instruction.

>   Interrupts are delivered "between" instructions, not during one.

That is implementation dependent. Often (particularly in CISC processors or
systems with long pipelines) an interrupt may occur while an instruction is
in flight. On a system with virtual memory and memory-memory operations like
the 68000 or VAX this may actually be common. There are various mechanisms for
dealing with it:

	1. Dump information about the current internal state on the stack,
	   restore it and resume the instruction later.

	2. Back out the instruction and undo any side effects, then start
	   it over after the interrupt is handled.

	3. Defer the interrupt until the instruction is complete (this is
	   obviously not an option for VM page faults :->).

	4. Abort the instruction, and let software fix things up and resume
	   the instruction or emulate it and continue afterwards.

	5. Provide a secondary processor to handle interrupts (One company,
	   I think it was Altos, did this to get demand paging on a 68000:
	   they ran two CPUs in lockstep and had the "trailing" CPU take
	   over and handle page faults.

-- 
