David,

The price of Windows loaded on a PC is in fact not buried many layers deep.
It can't be because of the way MRP systems based on SAP control inventory
and costing and markup ricing for large international corporations.

You see, the actual software kit that comes with the little Windows book and
the Windows NT CD-ROM with the actual Windows NT bits, has a shelf value of
$8.50 or so. Microsoft assess the surtax of Windows against the actual
number of PC's shipped, thus, the actual costing of the Windows surtax must
be levied against the product just prior to shipping it to a distributor or
putting it into the channels. As such, there is a legal need to provide
tangible eveidence that the valuable bits are in place on the system in
question and this is done by sticking a small license sticker on the side of
the computer with the Windows license on it. That little sticker found on
the side of the Compaq systems enclosure in fact has a value of a few
hundred dollars as an inventoriable item.

rtt




