Take a look at other posts in this thread.   There is a trick that you
can prefix a name with a character or somesuch.   You also have to
make sure that the 'quotes' propagate correctly.

For example, a filename like 'a a.c b' needs to have the quotes
managed correctly.   I just did a test like the following:

cp foo 'f o.x x'
or
cp foo "f o.x x"

For constant values, these quotes are the same.  For shell variables, the
results would be different.

and the filename was correctly created.   When scripting, it is important
to quote, and sometimes to use shell variables to contain
filenames.   When storing a filename into a shell variable, make sure that
there is a controlled number of quotes (most often, none.)   When using
a shell variable, use the appropriate quoting for the entire name, or
use the appropriate scheme for slicing off the portion of the name that
you are interested in.

When doing comparisons, there are also some methods as shown in
other postings on this thread.   In ksh (or other normal shells), the
difference between '', "" and other kinds of quoting need to be well
understood.

John



