In article <z4QZ7.679$Kf.12054@ord-read.news.verio.net>,
 <robert@bonomi.invalid> wrote:
>Whereupon, one is either into 'context-sensitive' wild-card expansion (where
>"*.foo *.bar" means one thing to 'wc', and something else to 'copy'), or 
>silliness like  "(.foo|.bar)"  for 'anything that matches either suffix'.

A lot of Amiga programs didn't accept "#?.foo #?.bar" for that
reason, you actually had to use "#?.foo|#?.bar" (I think that was
the syntax).

Another reason for handling wildcards the UNIX way is security. If
a a program you're passing a string to is not going to interpret
the string as shell level metadata, you don't need to try and
duplicate the metadata semantics of the shell/DCL/whatever to keep
someone from slipping an exploit through.

Of course this doesn't always work, especially when you're using
a scripting language like Perl that doesn't let you build an argv
explicitly. One reason I prefer using Tcl for CGI even if it's a
bit of a hog.

