VMS will happily take both foo.bar.too and foo^.bar.too  - depending on the
context you may need the "^" because old code might not like the non
standard characters.  The dot isn't an interesting one - but say a colon is
another story.  You can have a valid file name called "foo:bar" but in
standard VMS filename syntax, the colon is a device seperator - so instead
you need "foo^:bar.".  This allows the standard VMS utilities and command
file interpretor to handle the names.  An application using C that uses UNIX
semantics on the other hand will see and use "foo:bar" as it expected.

The output from the DIR can be used as input into any normal VMS utility -
including the "^" characters... even if they might also take the same name
without the "^" character.


