Mark Crispin wrote in message ...
>On Fri, 4 Jan 2002, Fred Kleinsorge wrote:
>> The "^"
>> character is not in the filename, but is displayed this way for
>> compatability with existing OpenVMS applications.
>
>But by your own admission, the display of "^" is modal.  Thus there is no
>way of knowing whether "foo^.bar.zap" means the file "foo.bar.zap" or the
>file "foo^.bar.zap".
>


The display of foo^.bar.zap from the directory command is always identical
to
foo.bar.zap  - it never means foo^^^.bar.zap

>Nor is there any way to convey the name "foo^.bar.zap" unambiguously to
>another program, since "foo^^.bar.zap" and "foo^^^.bar.zap" can mean other
>file names.
>

This is true ONLY in the case of a C application that has enabled explicit
UNIX filename syntax compatability.  Which right now, is ONLY done on some
COE applications (since it's brand new).   So given any existing VMS
application, the name is unambiguous.

The only ambiguity would be if the USER typed the name foo^.bar.zap when
what he really meant was foo^^^.bar.zap

The fact that the user can enter the name foo.bar.zap is only because it is
unambiguous as well as being equivalent to foo^.bar.zap as input.

Code that sees the files without the escape characters tends to be external
(non-VMS) applications running pathworks, ftp, or nfs to look at a file on a
VMS disk.  In which case the escape characters never come into play, because
these applications are NT/UNIX applications accessing a VMS disk file.







