  Ah, the perpetual "Porting OpenVMS to IA-32" question.  Nice to see 
  this topic is back under discussion again -- it's a discussion that 
  has been sorely missed, and one that was probably getting lonely. :-)

In article <uzo3ukws2.fsf@graphics.cornell.edu>, westin*nospam@graphics.cornell.edu (Stephen H. Westin) writes:
:Peter Flass <peter_flass@yahoo.com> writes:
:
:> If I'm following this thread correctly, the problems with VMS are:
:>   1. Compaq owns it and apparently doesn't want to release it.
:>   2. Most of it (AFAIK) is written in a proprietary language BLISS-32.

  The OpenVMS Bliss compilers and documentation are available on the
  Freeware.  (Though not the source code, which seems to be part of 
  the underlying request.)

:>      Unless you want to rewrite all of VMS, you'd have to port BLISS
:>      which (again AFAIK) is not intended to be portable.

  The Bliss language was deliberately intended to be very portable.
  (Packages and code were regularly ported between the various older
  DIGITAL operating systems, for instance.)

:Worse. Almost all of VMS was written in VAX assembly code. 

  All?  News to me.  Bliss is a substantial chunk of the early codebase.
  At present, OpenVMS is split into thirds between Macro32, Bliss, and C,
  with various other languages.

:...To port to
:Alpha, DEC wrote a *compiler* to generate Alpha code from VAX assembly
:code.

  Well, it was either that or translate and debug tracts of existing 
  source code.  The compiler approach was easily the most elegant 
  approach to porting the code.
 
:     I'm sure some modules were recoded for the Alpha.

  Very few modules were recoded, actually.

:                                                       But anyway,
:another compiler would need to be written to take VMS to another
:hardware platform. For starters.

  So?

:I think some utilities (perhaps including RMS) were written in
:BLISS-32, which was developed in the pious hope that it would be
:portable. I don't know how successful that was; the language might
:have been a bit too close to the hardware. But this code has
:presumably been ported to Alpha, which means it is portable.

  Bliss is portable and found across a number of platforms (RSX-11,
  RT-11, Wintel, though Bliss as an implementation language never 
  caught on.

:So we need two compilers to start the process (after Compaq releases
:source, which will probably never happen): VAX assembly and
:BLISS.

  C, C++, SDL and a host of other compilers and build tools and
  supporting tools are needed.  (I haven't run a count of the lines
  of code in OpenVMS Alpha recently, but I'd guess the current count 
  should be somewhere between 27 and probably 35 million lines.)

: Since these were written after the advent of modern
:compiler-writing tools (unlike, say, the Multics PL/I compiler), there
:is a chance that it wouldn't be too painful to change the code
:generators.

  Bliss, Macro32 and other compilers use the GEM code generator on
  Alpha.  Work is underway to move the compilers to the Intel IA-64
  code generator as part of the port of OpenVMS to Itanium, and 
  particularly to preserve the language extensions.


