On Fri, 4 Jan 2002 09:07:17 +0800, Julius
<julius-kl_goh@hp.com> wrote:
> This is my first foray into the Alpha word and I have a couple of questions
> on the Digital PWS 500a which I am trying to upgrade and install Linux.
> Could some kind soul help answer my questions?  I presume the answers to
> these questions apply to the 500au, 600a and 600au as well.
> 
> 1)  Can I upgrade to a faster processor like a 533Mhz or 600Mhz 21164 ?  If
> yes, are there any jumper setting?

yes, search google for 'alpha overclocking' and you'll find a site where
jumper-settings are explained. I'm sure, however, that you won't notice
the difference between a 500 and a 600 unless you pick a stopwatch. If
you want it for scientific computations that last days, you'd better get
something faster anyway. I wouldn't bother. If you want a real fast
alpha, get a 21264. More expensive, though - an XP1000 went for
something like US$ 900 last time on ebay.

As for speed, my 500au is really fast enough for day-to-day work,
compiling kernels etc. Compiling Mozilla takes quite long. Decoding
DVD's is not realtime :-(

> 
> 2)  Can I use PC based non-ECC SDRAM DIMMs?

my 500au needs ECC. Either buy a known-good brand (Crucial was
mentioned) or take a gamble and buy some on Ebay. I don't regret Ebay -
1.5 Gb for $200 :-)

> 
> 3)  Will a PC based SCSI controller like the Adaptec 29160 Ultra 160
> controller work on this machine?  If not, which controllers are supported?
> 
Everything with QLogic or Symbios Logic/NCR chipsets is a good bet. I'm
sure Linux will work with the 29160, but the Alpha-bios needs to
recognize it too. There's ARC, the NT-bios, which may recognize it, and
there's SRM, the unix-bios, which probably won't. Check the
hardware-compatibility lists at http://www.alphalinux.org (or
linuxalpha, one of them is often down).

Good luck,
Jurriaan
