The design goal is binary hardware compatibility, to stay VAX OS
independent. You can also run VAXeln, NetBSD etc. No extra files needed.

VMS folders are typically mapped to a Windows disk drive (or network share)
but remain in VMS format. Other options exist; you can actually reconnect an
entire VAX SCSI disk to the Windows host via a SCSI controller and boot VMS
from it.

There are a number of extensions, like internal Ethernet, allowing a
connection between the VAX environment and a process on the host (and
multiple instances of CHARON-VAX running as a cluster on a single SMP host
system). We also provide F-11, an extension of the Windows explorer which
allows cut and paste both ways between a VAX disk image and the Windows
environment (while the emulator is running), with implicit format
conversion. None of these need additional files to be loaded in VMS.

