On Fri, 4 Jan 2002, Fred Kleinsorge wrote:
> >>    1) make sure it's an Alpha, VAXen don't do ODS-5
> >Strike one.  It isn't a VMS feature, it's an Alpha VMS feature.
> What is your point?  You have a lot of new VAXes you are using for new
> development?

It's a waste of a good Alpha to run VMS on it.

Then again, Compaq so badly broke advfs in OSF/1 that people are giving up
on Alphas, so I guess some retrocomputing fan may want to intercept one on
the way to the dumpster to run VMS.

> >>    2) a privileged user changes you from ODS-2 to ODS-5:
> >>       set volume /structure_level=5 disk:
> >Strike two.  It isn't a user-accessible feature, it's something that a god
> >user has to do.
> Usually the volume is created as ODS-5, so unless you are migrating to a new
> disk structure - this is not interesting.

So what happens if you make a distribution archive and try to restore it
to one of the old filesystems?  Horrible error 69?

> >Strike three.  There's this random ^ character inserted in the output
> >name.
> To preserve backward compatability, VMS will display and allow files with
> "special" characters using the ^ as an escape (like C with a backslash).
> You don't *need* to include it on input.

Which means that you can't have compatiblity with names that have those
"special" characters.  This also means that a script which may use the
output from DIR as well as other sources for file names has no way of
knowing what is actually intended.

> And if you were using the filename
> in a C application, using C calls, you can get the name without the ^ (by
> indicating with a logical that you want UNIX filename semantics).

See above.  How can a script which takes filenames from the output of
another program know, precisely, what foo^.bar.baz means?  Is it a file
named "foo.bar.baz" or a file named "foo^.bar.baz"?

Presumably, to make a file named "foo^.bar.baz" you would need to create
"foo^^.bar.baz".  Or is it "foo^^^.bar.baz"?  How then does the program
clearly convey its intention to subsequent programs?

Yes, I know.  You don't do this sort of advanced scripting on VMS.

TOPS-20's CTRL/V (which served the purpose of ^) was pretty bad too, but
at least it was unambiguous.  There was only one way that a name could be
generated, and only one possible interpretation for an input name.

VMS, on the other hand, seems to have the worst of both with this kludge.

In any case, why should "foo^.bar.baz" be different from "foo.bar^.baz"?
Why is there something special about a dot that is unquoted, unless you
are trying to cling to the old notion that a "filename" and an "extension"
are two entirely separate fields?  TOPS-20 was roasted for this, and the
critics were right; the OS has no business making this distinction.

It also looks like "*" breaks on "." on VMS.  Or maybe it stops on some
"." but not other?

Hey, don't blame me.  You built this kludge tower.

> >VMS is out.  But thank you for playing.
> VMS is out with you regardless.  Is someone trying to convince you
> otherwise?

Digital sure tried pretty hard to do so back in 1983.  And now, with the
long-overdue demise of VMS at hand, we have the spectacle of VMS groupies
blaming people who went to UNIX instead of supporting VMS.

It's so lovely to hear that in the 21st century, VMS development is
addressing design flaws first observed in the late 1970s and early 1980s.
So when is VMS going to have CTRL/T?  Recognition and completion,
including partial completion?  What about "?" (sheesh, even Microsoft does
a better job!)?

Or is it still the policy of VMS development that these will "never be
done because they are inefficient"???

