In article 
<Pine.NXT.4.50.0201040826560.21933-100000@Tomobiki-Cho.CAC.Washington.EDU>,
 Mark Crispin <mrc@CAC.Washington.EDU> wrote:

> In any case, why should "foo^.bar.baz" be different from "foo.bar^.baz"?
> Why is there something special about a dot that is unquoted, unless you
> are trying to cling to the old notion that a "filename" and an "extension"
> are two entirely separate fields?  TOPS-20 was roasted for this, and the
> critics were right; the OS has no business making this distinction.

The OS does have a business making a distinction between a file name and 
metadata about what sort of file it is.  Treating the metadata as an 
extension of the file name isn't great, but the Unix strategy of not having 
any metadata is even worse.

There's a good discussion of this issue in
http://www.arstechnica.com/reviews/01q3/metadata/metadata-1.html
