On 3 Jan 2002, Rob Young wrote:
> 	Never had that problem with VMS as a new
> 	file/version is automatically created.

Strange to say, the VMS port of vi doesn't write a new version.  It just
always updates version 1.

Maybe it has something to do with being an editor that is designed to
write in place, as opposed to an artiface of the host operating system.
There's actually a reason why vi works that way.

I haven't used such editors in 22 years, and I haven't had to.

VMS file versioning is a PITA because it versions when you don't want it
to.  What's more, it has no auto-delete.  Probably because, like UNIX, VMS
has no undelete.  On TOPS-20, you could set the version retention to 1,
allow the backup versions to be deleted, but always have the option to
undelete a backup version.

But then again, both TOPS-20 and VMS are dead.  The only difference is
that people still care about TOPS-20 after all these years.  Nobody will
care about VMS 18 years from now.

