"Terry C. Shannon" wrote:
> First, the whole of the public is not the target audience for OpenVMS. (Or
> MVS or NSK, etc).

Yes they are. For reasons similar to why itis important to bring back VMS into
the education sector. It has to be seen as a popular serious server OS to
counter all the PR damage that was done to it over the years.

Someone at a company must suggest "have you looked at Compaq's VMS to compare
?" for that company to look into it and see if it might be a viable solution.

If I were hired at a company that didn't have any VMS, I would make sure NOT
TO PUSH VMS. It would make ME look bad, legacy etc etc for suggesting a
company buy some old dying OS.

Compaq must make VMS look moderns and popular if its wants the VMS loyalists
to be able to push it outside of the few remaining VMS shops.

Lets face it: Compaq isn't pushing VMS, it is the VMS loyalists who are
pushing it (and has been the case historically). The problem is that VMS
loyalists are either staying put in the few remaining VMS jobs, or changing
carreers completely and jumping into NT/UNIX to give them access to many more
jobs. They have stopped tryong to spread VMS to non VMS jobs because doing so
make them less amrketable for jobs when they must convince their employers
that they have dropped VMS and embraced Unix/NT.

The Public visibility of VMS won't generate sales directly, but it is still a
necessary step to put VMS back into the race.


