In article <slrna341ak.85t.kd5ob@charlie.ebertlan.org>,
Charlie Ebert <kd5ob@charlie.ebertlan.org> wrote:
> 10 years ago, FreeBSD was a professional OS when Linux was barely filling
> a single floppy disk.

Well, actually, ten years ago neither 386BSD nor Linux was what you'd
consider a complete OS. 386BSD was in the process of being turned from
a "purified" 4.3BSD release tape to an OS by Bill Jolitz and a horde
of hackers, and Linux was a "super Minix".

> Given you consider 'Open Source' the issue here, since both OS's had
> 'Open Source', what was the deciding factor which made Linux eclipse
> BSD then?  What was it?  Tell us?

The AT&T lawsuit, which was settled because it turned out AT&T had violated
the BSD license when they incorporated parts of BSD in System V without
giving credit, and the University of California countersued. But in the
meantime, BSD lost a lot of momentum and Linux, which was clearly AT&T
free, got the wide distribution first.

