In article <a0v555INNsn0@subds.rzg.mpg.de>,
Bruce Scott TOK <bds@rzg.mpg.de> wrote:
> Convince me that the free BSD movements pre-date GNU and I might alter
> my stance.

Brian Kernighan, P. J. Plauger, _Software Tools_, Addison Wesley, 1976. The
source code to Ratfor and all the other tools in the book was distributed on
magnetic tape, and could be freely redistributed. The Software Tools User
Group at Berkeley was formed as a result of this book, and the Berkeley
based "Free Software" movement dates back to this event.

> But it is clear RMS's stance came out of that MIT milieu of
> the late 60s/early 70s,

Actually, *that* stance goes back millenia. You can't credit RMS as the only
anointed son of the traditional university environment. It's clear that things
were not so different in Berkeley in the '60s and '70s... nor at the University
of New South Wales, nor (I imagine) were they that much different at any
other university.

Outside the University, but nearby, the People's Computer Company was founded
in 1972. In 1976 they started publishing Doctor Dobbs' Jounral of Computer
Calisthenics and Orthodontia. Within a few years several microcomputer
versions of "real" operating systems and languages were published... and
this included the first generally available compiler for a subset of "C".

All of these ideas were boiling out of the luminiferous ether all over the
world at around the same time, and to me RMS' greatest impact has been to
unnecessarily polarise the left and right coast variants of the same
movement... whatever you call it.

