In article <Pine.NXT.4.50.0201031115260.19934-100000@Tomobiki-Cho.CAC.Washington.EDU>, Mark Crispin <mrc@CAC.Washington.EDU> writes:

>VMS file versioning is a PITA because it versions when you don't want it
>to.  What's more, it has no auto-delete.  Probably because, like UNIX, VMS
>has no undelete.  On TOPS-20, you could set the version retention to 1,
>allow the backup versions to be deleted, but always have the option to
>undelete a backup version.

Wrong.  (Or outdated, but this has been around for a long time, and I get 
the feeling that a lot of your specific complaints about VMS are about 1.0; 
no reason you should get familiar with the current capabilities of an OS you
hate, but it might be reasonable to qualify some of your statements with "when
I last looked at VMS" or some such.) Anyway,

$ SET FILE/VERSION_LIMIT=n filespec

tells RMS to retain only as many versions of the particular file as you 
specify.  You can have 0 old versions or up to 32766.

No OS-provided undelete though (and the freeware undeletes are only likely
to work if you quiesce the disk immediately after the mistaken delete, except
for the SAFETY package, which needs to be installed and working before the
deletion you want to reverse).

