Bob Koehler wrote:
> 
>  Michael Zarlenga wrote:
> >
> > This 15+yr VMS user who also has 2+yrs of Unix finds Unix much
> > easier to use for most of the tasks I do.  I really hate to say
> > that, but its scripting languages are light years beyond DCL.
> 
>    So how do I do the following in you favorite shell (yes, I do know
>    two painfull answers)?
> 
>    rename *.a *.b

Why don't you show us how to make

	rename *.a.part2 *.b.part1

work on VMS?  Because you can't. The file systems are different,
and VMS doesn't allow multiple periods in a file name, while
UNIX does.

There are actually many ways to do this using a shell script, but it 
will require more typing than on VMS.  The UNIX file system does not
consider things after a decimal point to be a separate part of the file 
name like VMS does, and thus doesn't have built in rules to change 
individual "pieces" of the name.

If I wanted to prove the superiority of UNIX in the same fashion,
I could ask how would you do the following on VMS

	mv my_file.one my_file.one.part1

Answer: You would have to punt.  VMS only allows one decimal point in
a file name.  You would have to change one of the decimal points into
some other character.  Does this prove UNIX superiority? I doubt it.
You could also play games with the characters UNIX accepts in a file
name but VMS does not.  It's a stupid comparison.

Anyway, this is a rarely needed operation on either system.  The real
question should be "why do you have to change the names?"
If you are trying to hide the files, why not just put them in a sub 
directory where you don't have to mangle the names.
If they have the wrong extension on them, why?  Find the person who 
gave them the wrong name in the first place, and beat the 
&#^%& out of him.  Maybe he won't do it again.

Each OS has it's good and bad points. Use whichever one that fits the 
task.  Picking one operation to "prove" the superiority of one over
the other is silly and stupid.  You might just as well say UNIX is 
better because you can type "who" on it, while VMS forces you to
type the painfully longer "show user".  It's a stupid comparison.



