In the mid to late 80's, Multics was ported to an Intel platform under
the name of Opus by some group in New York (I believe).  This was
after HIS had basically given up the claim to the Multics OS (sold
it).  To the best of my knowledge, Opus was never completed to the
point of being a released product.

The current XTS product is the grandson of SCOMP, which started with
STOP 2.1 on custom built hardware. Each name change (SCOMP to XTS 200
to XTS 300 to the to-be-released XTS 400) reflects a significant
change in both OS architecture and hardware platform. The XTS 200 was
on a HIS/Bull DPS 6000, the XTS 300 on Intel x86 systems. The XTS 400
will be released on unannounced Xeon supporting 2GHz+ supporting
systems with a Linux-compatible API.  The lineage is as follows:

SCOMP with STOP 2.1 (12/1984)
First A1 Orange Book evaluated system

XTS 200 STOP 3.1.E (5/1992)
First General Purpose B3 evaluated platform
XTS 200 RAMP to STOP 3.2.E (3/1994)

XTS 300 RAMP to STOP 4.1.A (5/1995) and update (10/1995)
Intel (486) based with outboard communications subsystems
Intel Binary Compatible and BSD API

XTS 300 Evolution
PI RAMP to STOP 4.4.2  133/166 Mhz processor and onboard TCP/IP
(3/1998)
PII/PIII RAMP to STOP 5.2.E - 333/500 Mhz Dual processor (5/2000)
first OS to receive evaluation when connected to two different level
networks

XTS 400 with STOP 6.0 
Linux API to enter Common Criteria/NIAP evaluation by end of 2002 at
EAL4 or higher.

As of today, the XTS 300 is on the EPL at the B3 level, and is very
much in production and operational use by hundreds of customers using
many accredited applications.

STOP 6.0, the Linux API OS intended for the XTS 400, is today
demonstrable and will be released in a limited BETA form by mid-2002
when the Intel hardware is finally available.  STOP 6.0 imitates Linux
but doesn't use Linux kernel code.

Please contact Mike Focke (Mike.Focke@GetronicsGov.com)with any
questions or interest in the product.


