You may indeed be so bold !

My first thoughts are to get the information in front of the people who
matter, the CIO's.  Hit them with the technical features of VMS and
explain what these features will enable them to achieve. These are the
people who make the IT spending decisions.  Make it difficult for them
to ignore the advantages of VMS.

I would start out with magazines like "CIO" and similar pulications that
are relevant at the top of the IT food-chain.  Over time work up and
down to provide similiar information to CEO's and to the general IT
professional.

One thing that is important is to get the press releases out there for
the professionals who look at these things and for the IT media so they
will start to make the existence of VMS common knowledge.

Superbowls and blimps for VMS ?  More advertising on busstops in Poland
?  I don't think so.  We are trying to target the small percentage of
people who work in IT and can make use of the information.

TV campaigns ?  Maybe - but only during business programs watched by
senior execs, again because this is the target audience.

Actually Compaq would do themselves a big service by advertising all
their high-end products in these ways.  The broad scatter advertising to
the home (or small business) user of PCs is fine because that is the
market.  The high-end stuff needs to be directed at IT professionals and
IT decision makers but it is important to note that today's programmers
may one day be the decision-makers and they need to be aware of VMS too
if VMS is to have any real longevity. 


Okay ?


John McLean


