I not only can, but *DO* understand why someone would prefer shell
programming to DCL - for those tasks which are doable on UN*X.

As someone in another thread mentioned, you cannot

# mv *.a *.b

...in UN*X. On VMS, this technique can be used to reset version numbers
back to a sequence beinning with 1 (the whole version number concept is
likewise foreign to UN*X), as well as for other reasons.

> For example, the point you are trying to make is simply meaningless on
> UNIX.

Well, no it isn't. It is both intrinsic and important.

> You do not write scripts in the shell by itself, ever.

Well, actually yes you do. "ls" cannot execute an "if" construct or
setup a pipeline anymore than the shell can return the creation date,
revision date or other statistic about a file without assistance from an
external command program.

Other externals may execute scripts as well; however, the shell script
is "the thingama-bob that does the job", and yes, it *IS* executed by
the shell, and only the shell. Period.

> It doesn't
> make sense to try and compare the shell without any external programs to
> DCL or Perl or anything else.

Well actually yes it does. In fact, you can liken it to invoking VMS
system services or RTL ropuines in any of your choice of 2/3GLs.

> A UNIX shell script always uses a combination
> of the shell and the filters that it runs...

Well, at least on that much we can agree.

> Would you complain because the PDP-11 doesn't have a MID$ instruction? Oh,
> sure, you can call EN EXTERNAL SUBROUTINE to get a MID$, but wouldn't it
> be better to program in BASIC where MID$ is built in?

Well, in the first place, MID$ would create a rather long sequence of
instructions or a call to the associated STR$ routine (on VMS). So,
comparing a "macro" to an "atomic" instruction is hardly logical.

> That sort of argument is obviously nonsense. Well, that's the case with the
> shell as well.

Well, the example is nonsense. The concept is not.

I've seen commonly in other VMS 3GLs use of BAS$ RTL modules in such
languages as COBOL, DIBOL, etc.

You may want to broaden your EDP horizons a bit. Your paradigm indicates
somewhat limited experience.

