I'm sorry, but the bit you keep harping on is completely and utterly
central to the whole system. There's no point even having a separate
shell if you isolate it from the filters.

># mv *.a *.b

The canonical "this is the one thing you can do in VMS you can't do in UNIX"
example shows up, at last.

Not only can you do that in UNIX, you can do it a dozen different ways.

ls *.a | sed 's/\(.*\)\.a/mv & \1.b/' | sh -x

for i in *.a; do mv $i "`expr $i : '\(.*\)\.a'`"; done

It's about the only thing that you can do in DCL that requires less
typing than the shell, and there are about a dozen utilities available
to "fix" this "problem"... and I've never seen anyone ever use it after
verifying it works.

>> Would you complain because the PDP-11 doesn't have a MID$ instruction? Oh,
>> sure, you can call EN EXTERNAL SUBROUTINE to get a MID$, but wouldn't it
>> be better to program in BASIC where MID$ is built in?

>Well, in the first place, MID$ would create a rather long sequence of
>instructions or a call to the associated STR$ routine (on VMS).  So,
>comparing a "macro" to an "atomic" instruction is hardly logical.

So why do you see that this kind of comparison is illogical and
meaningless in one case, but not another?

>I've seen commonly in other VMS 3GLs use of BAS$ RTL modules in such
>languages as COBOL, DIBOL, etc.

But (to paraphrase you) that's a RTL module, not the instruction set! We
need to compare BASIC and the PDP-11 instruction set on the basis of its
inherent capabilities, and the PDP-11 instruction set doesn't have a MID$
instruction! What happens if your RTL isn't installed?

