On 2 Jan 2002, Bob Koehler wrote:
>    So how do I do the following in you favorite shell (yes, I do know
>    two painfull answers)?
>    rename *.a *.b

This can be done with a small shell script, but yes, it is more complex on
UNIX than it is on DOS, VMS, or TOPS-10/20.

On the other hand, unlike DOS, VMS, or TOPS-10/20, there's hardly any
reason to do it on UNIX.  UNIX filenames are not restricted to the
familiar file.extension form; the "." character is just an ordinary
character in a file name.  Equally important, subdirectories are much
easier to use in UNIX than in VMS or TOPS-10/20.

Simply put, there are other (and better) ways on UNIX to accomplish what
is intended by that rename.  The only people who ever ask how to do that
type of rename are people who come from environments which use a strict
file.extension naming and haven't yet comprehended that there are better
ways to do what the underlying intention.  Typically, even after they are
provided with a prepackaged script to do that rename, they end up not
using it since it turns out that doing things the UNIX way is so much
easier.

You can see the same difference with DOS vs. NT.  Even though the command
interface is similar and NT has the DOS/VMS/TOPS-10/TOPS-20 type of
rename, people hardly ever use it since NT filenames work like UNIX.

-- Mark --

