Peter da Silva wrote:
> [snip]
> But (to paraphrase you) that's a RTL module, not the instruction set!

EXACTLY! It's a SOFTware thing, not HARDware! *VERY* important
distinction. The same HARDware stuff will be found on every PDP CPU,
every VAX CPU, every Alpha CPU, every IPF CPU, etc. ...

If you're confused here also, you may need to do computer school all
over again.

> We
> need to compare BASIC and the PDP-11 instruction set on the basis of its
> inherent capabilities, and the PDP-11 instruction set doesn't have a MID$
> instruction! 

MID$() is a BASIC built-in function. This is a far-cry from a machine
instruction. BASIC is a 3GL, not a 2GL. The whole comparison is totally
nonsensical and irrelevant to this discussion.

In an older BASIC (BASIC-Plus), MID$() could even be stated on the left
of an "=" (as the target of an assignment) to replace a string segment
in place. As it happens, (on VMS) there's a STR$ routine that performs a
similar function (I believe).

> What happens if your RTL isn't installed?

BASRTL is distributed with OpenVMS, not with the language (although the
DEC BASIC distro. may contain an updated version). If it weren't, BASIC
routines and programs would not be usable from one system (the
developer's) to another (the end-user's). Same for DIBOL (DBLRTL), COBOL
(COBRTL), and so on. They all come with VMS.

Another of the VMS things you don't typically find in the UN*X world (or
WhineBloze, DOS, etc.).

