Mark Crispin wrote:
> Simply put, there are other (and better) ways on UNIX to accomplish what
> is intended by that rename.  The only people who ever ask how to do that
> type of rename are people who come from environments which use a strict
> file.extension naming and haven't yet comprehended that there are better
> ways to do what the underlying intention.

Unfortunately, while the "pure core" of Unix doesn't care what
an extension is, most all the tools - even really basic tools like
the C compiler - do care:

$ cat > hello.c
main() { printf("Hello world\n"); }
$ cc -o hello hello.c
$ ./hello
Hello world
$ mv hello.c hello.somethingelse
$ cc -o hello hello.somethingelse
/usr/bin/ld:hello.somethingelse: file format not recognized; treating as
linkert
/usr/bin/ld:hello.somethingelse:1: parse error
collect2: ld returned 1 exit status

Yes, I know that there is a flag that tells the C compiler to
treat the filename as a C source code file no matter what it's
extension.  And that it's not really a flag to the C compiler but
to the compiler/loader driver.  But my point remains: a Unix
programmer learns on day one that file extensions *do* matter to
all the most interesting Unix tools.

Tim.


