"Larry Kilgallen" <Kilgallen@SpamCop.net> wrote in message
news:1QZ6FklbbrHE@eisner.encompasserve.org...

...

> I think "semantics similar to" is a far cry from "VAXcluster-compatible".
> To me the latter implies "plug-compatible".

That's how I interpreted the call for a 'clone' (i.e., as a *true* clone) as
well.  It would be much easier to create a VMS-like system (that was even an
improvement over the original in some respects) than to create a
source-level-compatible platform with identical system semantics (unless
VMS's owner were to take on the task).

Does the world need such a similar, better VMS?  Hard to say.  There's at
least a reasonable argument that compatibility with existing familiar
environments counts for at least as much as the feature set (witness
parallel discussions about IA64's deficiencies in this area when compared
with x86-64), and that improving (even at the cost of a complete rewrite)
Unix or NT would be a more productive approach (and Unixes have been moving
somewhat in this direction for at least a decade anyway).

Does the world need a true VMS clone on other hardware?  I'd say not, or at
least certainly not until VMS has shown the ability to rise from the
near-grave and prove that it is attractive to a much larger market than it
currently commands.  Which once again brings one back around to the need for
management with some willingness to explore such possibilities...

- bill





