Kilgallen@SpamCop.net (Larry Kilgallen) wrote in message news:<1QZ6FklbbrHE@eisner.encompasserve.org>...
> I think "semantics similar to" is a far cry from "VAXcluster-compatible".
> To me the latter implies "plug-compatible".

I wasn't trying to suggest that the IBM code could be used as-is. 
Rob's point was that a DLM implementation was one of the pieces of a
VMS clone that would be hard, if not impractical, to write.  I've
worked enough with the DLM that it doesn't seem so awfully hard to me,
and I was trying to point out that it had already been cloned.  In
fact, it's been done multiple times; if you recall, Oracle did a clone
for OPS.

If the semantics provided at the user level are the same, then the
internal implementation details shouldn't need to be plug-compatible. 
As I see it, a VMS clone doesn't necessarily need to be able to join a
real VMS cluster, if it can cluster with nodes of its own kind and
provide users with the same capabilities.  But having identical system
service interfaces (including $ENQ/$DEQ) would help a lot in allowing
people to move VMS software to it.
