> > would have helped to improve VMS's mindshare.

> > Why did universities abandon VMS ? Was cost ( hardware and OS )
> > the primary factor ? How important was source code availability ?

> Simply put!  You had to pay big bucks for VMS and you didn't for
> Unix.

> If they GPL'd VMS we might be running that, who knows.

Go back to the end of the 70s, to when VMS shipped. You payed AT&T
$200US, and you where set, for unis. A small extra chare to add extra
machine perhaps, can't remember. But unix was *not* the OS of choise
at all then! RT-11, RSTS, tops and bottoms, and a hoard of
others. Unix was just one of many.

Now, unis got from DEC; the Vax, but NO edu discount, and they had to
pay full biscuit for the OS! Now for many, the OS was a $0 gratis
throw in to the deal. That stopped. So instantly, depts looked at unix
plus a 780. The 780 *was* price compeditive with the others.  So you
got Wollongong uni porting unix to ... (help...) that was then
reported to the 780. The BSD folk fell on the 780 like a pack of
starving jackels, and soon BSD 4.0a..z march across the globe. And
march they did. $200 vs DECs price for VMS made it a fish in a barrel
day. Melbourne uni ordered 12 780s in one order. They ALL ran BSD.

Then the folks who did EE and CS degrees went on to work for the new
startup. So where did they get a OS? Take BSD, write whatever you need
to get your MMU to look like a 780 pager, and you are (almost) done.

There where now a steady stream of compiler and OS litterates, and they
ALL knew unix and C. A few knew of something else...

By about 82/83, the slip with the venuses, the Apollo and soon Sun and
HP boxes, and the Jupiter cancelling, cut a big hole in DECs campus
profile. The Vax was almost synonymous with 'slow' and the 10/20 folks
in some cases now hated DEC with a vengence. And still do.

SO DEC did the hard yards and in effect GAVE Sun and the rest of the
unix hoard their entre into the real world. Witness that Apollo's
DomianOS was killed of by unix and X. (X also being a largly Vax
effort in the beginning)

After that, the Vax never regained its performance lead, the Alpha was
the first system that did that, though Omega and N-Vax went close, and
where compeditive with their compeditors, but for lots, it was too
late. They where solidly Sun or HP or SGI or IBM/RS shops and it
needed a much bigger performance margin to shift them.

The other factor, is that I don't theink the Vax suceeded where DEC
expected it too. If you look at early version, it is obvious that it
was intended as an RSX follow on, but the group who really took to it
where the RSTS people. Not sure about this though...

But no, it was not quick, and it was not easy, but with a decade and
a half of steady work, DEC marketing and managment started their own
hanging. And provided the rope into the bargain.

