If you only give credit for GNU, that is far and away more than enough.
RMS inspired a lot of people with his philosophy and willingness to put
his money where his mouth was, including people who were much less
radical than he was.  I cannot describe the feeling when I read the GNU
manifesto (a few days after my first move to Sun OS) and realise that in
the ensuing years people had actually acted on it.  I know RMS is too
much for most people, who are really fearful of a world which would work
so differently than our corporate one does, but it is clear to me that
none of the Open Source movement as it exists now (note well: for me it
is still the Free Software movement, in the spirit Stallman set it up
in) would do so had RMS and GNU not taken that initial plunge.  I don't
believe for a second we'd have Linux in any recognisable form had GNU
not been there first for several years.

