> 
> Continuing use of VMS by educational institutions ( for educational
> activities and not for behind the scene accounting ) would have helped
> to improve VMS's mindshare.
> 
> Why did universities abandon VMS ? Was cost ( hardware and OS ) the
> primary factor ? How important was source code availability ?


When I was at Melbourne University (late 70's into 1980) the computer
science department actually wanted to teach students about operating
systems, not how to use the latest whizz-bang software that they seem to
do now.

At that time Unix was not only cheap to buy but Computer Science Dept
could get hold of a substantial amount of Unix source code for their Vax
780.  By replacing certain standard programs with purpose-written stuff,
it was possible to illustrate all kinds of things.  They could easily
show the impact of changes to the process scheduling algorithm or create
compilers written for some pseudo-code.

I also remember (as a student) writing code with a special routine in
it.  This routine would be activated at some random point in time by
some similar change to the standard software.  I can't remember whet we
were supposed to learn from it, maybe something about the nature of
ASTs....

Unix at that time was highly flexibly and very accessible to
universities, in part because it grew out of being an operating system
for teaching.  VMS was always a robust, commercial-grade operating
system, one for which tinkering was not an option.

The result of all this was a bunch of graduates who were quite familiar
with the warts-and-all characteristics of Unix.  Ultimately many of
those graduates made recommendations for IT purchases and the rest is
history.


John McL


