>>As the subject implies, I have a 600au system that has been running
>>Tru4 5.1 for a year, which for some reason now powers up to a start 
>>screen that says 600a and wants to boot NT (which of course is not there). 
>>Do I just need to re-install the firmware? 
>
>Don't think so.
>But upgrading the firmware to the latest and greatest doesn't seem to be
>a bad idea either.
>

If the battery on the MB is dead, the system will power up in ARC-mode (NT)
Replace the battery, and try again.

>>FWIW A student said that his son "pushed a button" and the system crashed
>>and wouldn't reboot. While pushing a button probably caused the crash,
>>I can't imagine that it caused the 600au -> 600a problem. If anyone can
>>confirm that it was just a coinicidence that his son was involved you
>>will make him feel a whole lot better! :)
>
As the firmware is loaded at power-on, it is not uncommon that this happens when rebooting a machine. We have had a fair number of PWS that has worked perfectly well for years, even with reboots, but after half a day of no power went into NT-land.

>An AU system does always believe it is an A system (at least on an 433au).
>There is no difference in the firmware between an AU and an A, only the
>add-ons like the disk-controller, graphics-controller and maybe the NIC
>(in the NT = A versions they are the cheaper ones; in the the VMS/dUNIX = AU
>they are the more expensive - but supported by VMS/dUNIX - ones).
>

Another difference is that (at least on some) the CD-rom is strapped as master on an AU, and slave on an A.

>For me it looks like, the console switched from SRM (VMS/dUNIX/Linux/*BSD)
>to AlphaBIOS (NT/Linux) mode. This can be caused by entering ARC at the
>SRM prompt ">>>" or by loosing the firmware setup (which shouldn't happen
>by pressing a button - but I've seen it happen by a power-down).
>
>So switch back to SRM from AlphaBIOS (via a menu entry - don't ask me where
>to find it, I quitted AlphaNT and AlphaBIOS/ARC years ago) and you should
>be done. Maybe you need to define the default boot device and the boot options
>(eg. A for dUNIX multiuser mode) and maybe NIC options (eg. FASTFD).
>
