No, most would assume that if the script contains "#!/bin/sh" it will be
compatable with the original Bourne Shell.  This would, of course, mean
it would work on ksh, pdksh, ash, zsh, bash, etc. as all of the known sh
clones are supersets and support all of the features of the original sh.
If you use features not supported by the original sh you should but the
actual name of the command you wrote the script for. (as is done for things
like perl and tcl which work the same way, but have no similaritt to the
other shells.)

|> 
|> True, especially w/Linux - but I stay away from systems where /bin/sh is
|> not the Bourne shell :)

Then what does Linux have to do with it??  sh is not the Bourne Shell under
Linux, it is BASH.  The original Bourne Shell was never released to the public
and thus only show up in officially licensed versions of Unix that have paid
for it.  All others use one of the clones.

It does make a difference.

bill

