In article <u31f97avcn2r55@news.supernews.com>,
John Vottero <John@mvpsi.com> wrote:
> This is a red herring (and you have the story slightly wrong).  In NT V4,
> the graphics subsystem was moved into the kernel.  Prior to that it ran as a
> separate process.  So in V4 it's running in kernel mode and prior to that it
> was running in user mode.  Some people like to throw this up as a change
> that made NT less reliable because if the graphics subsystem has a problem,
> it crashes the whole system since it's now running in kernel mode.

The failure mode I'm concerned about isn't GDI crashing the whole system, it's
GDI corrupting something in the kernel instead of crashing. If there's a bug
in a driver in 3.51 it's more likely to cause a crash, and get fixed during
development. In 4.0 A memory corruption bug in a driver may go unnoticed for
a long time, and of course it's a lot harder to debug corruption problems
because first of all you have to figure out the subsystem that caused the
original error...

And, it took (IIRC) 4 service packs before NT 4 was as stable a server as
NT 3.51. I find it hard to believe that moving GDI into the kernel wasn't
involved...

