Gunshannon <bill@cs.uofs.edu> writes:
> > On Mon, 31 Dec 2001, David J. Dachtera wrote:
> >
> >>
> >> I believe I recall a post in comp.os.vms about some app. that created
> >> some 500,000 files a day. Maybe it's only 50,000 or something, but it
> >> *WAS* an outrageous number.
> >
> > I don't know enough about the internals of VMS but if this were on a
> > Unix system I still wouldn't put them all in one directory. I would
> > break them up by hour or even smaller increments if necessary.  I would
> > expect that a simple linear traverse of a directory on VMS would get
> > less and less practical just as it would on Unix as the size of that
> > directory grew.
>
> I talked to somebody from DEC once who knew how to solve this in
> some future version of VMS (or so he thought).
>
> Priority of such a fix is debatable, since people who write large
> applications know how to avoid such issues, regardless of what their
> primary operating system might be.

And if their operating system is VMS, it offers at least one pretty obvious
alternative (RMS indexed files) that was *designed* for that kind of thing
(unlike the ODS-2 directory structure, which was simply designed to handle
the sizes of directories generated - usually by humans - for categorization
purposes).  While it would be nice to have b-tree-structured directories (as
at least a couple of Unixes do) to handle (with reasonable performance) the
borderline cases where it may be more convenient to use the file system,
blaming the file system for the lack of such optimizations is rather like
blaming TECO for its performance short-comings as a general-purpose
programming language.

- bill





