On 1 Jan 2002 15:35:35 -0000, Doc.Cypher
<Use-Author-Supplied-Address-Header@[127.1]> wrote:
>Nobody has to pay real money for VMS for hobbyist use! The licenses are
>free, just like Linux all you have to pay for is a distribution (or borrow
>one from your local VMS site).
>Try visiting http://www.montagar.com/hobbyist/index.html

I did and looked for a download link.

Instead it says:
"In order to register for licenses, you need to be a member of a
DECUS."

Doing this in Sydney is very much a nineteenth century experience:

" To join, simply view and print out the PDF-format (Adobe Acrobat)
membership form below, fill it in, and send it to the DECUS office.
Upon approval of membership, you will be notified and a membership
card will be provided. "

Assuming that DECUS approve my membership,  I then have to apply to
Compaq in writing, via snail mail :

"Upon your qualification for this license and your signature on this
 form, Compaq Computer Corporation ("COMPAQ") will grant you the
 right to use the VAX OpenVMS on a single computer"

In addition, I have to go through the whole painful process every
year.

" Your license will be granted upon the issuance of the license key. 
  Your rights to use the software and the license key are LIMITED TO
ONE  YEAR from date of issuance of the license key."

In constrast. I can have the ISO for Linux or NetBSD in 20 minutes 
(  I have a fastish connection ) of clicking on the right url.

In addition, it markedly restricts what I can do:

"Use of the Licensed Computer is ONLY FOR   NON-COMMERCIAL
USES (e.g., home use).  As such, you may not use the Licensed Computer
for any business purposes whatsoever, e.g., to develop applications
for resale, to do business accounting, etc."

So if I develop an application that turns out to be viable as a
product, then I am stuffed. If I want to use the box at work to run a
simple database package then I am stuffed. This is not a problem with
Linux or the various BSD distributions.

I  am sorry to complain. However, I believe that when a low profile os
like VMS is being promoted, these barriers are likely to turn away
most interested people.


