> Since that's exactly what it's supposed to do, and very little more, what
> *is* your point? DCL is a procedural programming language (albeit one with
> fairly primitive control structures) that sometimes runs external programs
> to perform commands. The UNIX shell is a nearly functional programming
> language that primarily controls the dataflow between external programs.

...and DCL is not? You've never seen a truly sophisticated DCL "script"
then.

> This model turns out to be extraordinarily powerful, and allows you to build
> quite sophisticated programs out of simple components. It's nothing like DCL,
> and looking at features of DCL and trying to find them in the shell, or to
> criticise the shell for lacking them, is like complaining that your new
> minivan doesn't come equipped with a movie theatre and grocery store.
> 
> >...but the original poster was comparing DCL to the scripting languages,
> >not the external utilities they may invoke. Important distinction.
> 
> The original poster was talking about the ease of programming using the
> UNIX shell compared to using DCL. Since you can't do anything in the shell
> without external programs, 

It would probably be more accurate to say that the shell, under the
direction of a script, controls how those external programs are used.
The actual "scripting" capability comes from the shell, not the CUSPs.
Hence, the importance of the distinction between the shell itself and
the CUSPs.

> it should be obvious to the most naive observer,
> at least at this late date in the discussion, that this distinction is
> purely an invention of yours and not something intended by the original
> poster.

Obviously, I cannot agree.

You may want to read in O'Reilly's "UNIX in a nutshell" by Daniel Gilly:
in Chapter 4 starting near the bottom of page 4-12, the section titled
"Built-in Commands (Bourne and Korn Shell)" (ISBN 1-56592-001-5)

The difference between internal and external commands was even
documented in the MS-DOS documentation, usually in the discussion of the
PATH environment variable. 

There are(were) other VMS CLIs besides DCL, few and obscure as they may
be (may have been). The difference between an external and a cliroutine
does not become apparent until one begins to study the DCL command
tables in depth. See http://www.djesys.com/vms/freevms/proglist.txt for
some examples.

So, no, I did not "invent" this distinction. It is possible, however,
that my understanding of it may be deeper than (or different from) your
own.

